const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── MIDDLEWARE PIPELINE ──────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m18", "lld_m18_t7", 7, "Design a Middleware Pipeline", "design-middleware-pipeline",
    ["case-study", "chain-of-responsibility", "composition", "framework"],
    "You are writing the request layer of a web framework. Product wants a timing middleware that logs how long the WHOLE downstream stack took, an auth middleware that can reject before anything else runs, a cache middleware that can answer without ever touching the handler, and one error middleware that catches exceptions thrown anywhere below it. Design the pipeline so all four are ordinary objects registered in a list — and explain why a for-loop over that list cannot express any of them.",
    "The load-bearing idea is the CONTINUATION: a middleware does not receive 'the next index', it receives NEXT — a zero-argument function that runs the entire rest of the pipeline and returns when that rest is done. That single signature turns a flat list into an ONION: code before next() runs on the way in, code after next() runs on the way out, and a try/finally around next() wraps everything below it. The elegant construction is a FOLD of the list back-to-front, each step capturing the already-built inner chain in a closure, so the pipeline is assembled once at startup and is just a function call at request time.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: the framework must let an application register an ordered list of MIDDLEWARE components, each of which sees every request and may (a) inspect or modify the request before the rest of the stack runs, (b) inspect or modify the response after the rest of the stack has run, (c) SHORT-CIRCUIT — produce a response itself and never invoke the rest, which is exactly what auth rejection, rate limiting, and a cache hit need, and (d) wrap the downstream call in error handling, so an exception thrown three layers down is caught by a middleware registered above. Registration order must be the semantic order: the first registered is the outermost, the last registered runs closest to the terminal handler. There is exactly one TERMINAL HANDLER at the bottom — the route handler — which has no next to call. Middleware must be composable into sub-pipelines (a router mounts a group of middleware under a path prefix), and a middleware must be able to run asynchronously without the pipeline forcing it to block. Non-functional constraints that shape the design: composition happens ONCE at application startup, not per request — a pipeline of twenty middleware must cost twenty function calls per request, not twenty list lookups plus allocation; middleware must be independently testable, which means a middleware may not know its own position or the length of the list; and the framework must be able to DETECT contract violations (calling next twice, never calling next, writing to a response that has already been committed) because those three bugs produce symptoms far away from their cause. Explicitly out of scope: the HTTP parser, routing itself, and the thread/event-loop model — the pipeline is agnostic to all three."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "MIDDLEWARE is the whole abstraction and it is one method: invoke(Context ctx, Next next). It knows nothing about which middleware precedes or follows it; its only lever on the rest of the world is calling next.run(). NEXT is a continuation — a functional interface with a single no-argument run() method — and it is the piece that carries the design. Crucially, Next is not 'the next middleware'; it is 'everything below me, already composed into one callable'. CONTEXT is the mutable per-request carcass: the request object, a response builder, a typed attribute bag for middleware-to-middleware handoff (the auth middleware puts the resolved principal there and the handler reads it), and the COMMITTED flag that makes double-write detectable. Making Context the single parameter — rather than passing request and response separately — is what lets middleware add state without changing every signature. HANDLER is the terminal: handle(Context ctx), no next, one per route. PIPELINE is the builder and the composer: use(Middleware) appends to an ordered list, and build(Handler terminal) folds that list into a single Handler. The built object is IMMUTABLE and shared across all requests — no per-request state lives in it, which is why it can be constructed once. RESPONSEGUARD is a small but load-bearing collaborator: it owns the committed flag and throws IllegalStateException on a write after commit, converting a silent corruption into a stack trace pointing at the offending middleware. Optional: PIPELINEBUILDER for named/mounted sub-pipelines, which are themselves just Middleware — a sub-pipeline satisfies the same interface, so composition is closed under itself."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The pattern is CHAIN OF RESPONSIBILITY, but the textbook version is the wrong one and the difference is the whole interview. Classic CoR gives each handler a pointer to its successor and each handler either handles the request or forwards it — control never comes back. That models a filter chain, not a middleware pipeline: it cannot express 'log the duration of everything below me' because there is no 'after'. The middleware variant replaces the successor pointer with a CONTINUATION passed as an argument, and the moment control returns from next.run() you are on the way out of the onion. That single change buys the before/after symmetry, the try/finally wrap, and short-circuit for free — short-circuit is simply not calling next. The construction is a right FOLD: start with the terminal handler as the innermost callable, walk the middleware list BACKWARD, and at each step create a new Handler closure that calls middleware.invoke(ctx, () -> inner.handle(ctx)). After the fold, build() returns one Handler; at request time the framework calls it and the JVM's call stack IS the pipeline. Compare the obvious alternative, the INDEX-WALKING DISPATCHER: keep the list, keep a per-request cursor, and hand each middleware a Next that increments the cursor and dispatches to list.get(i+1). It produces the same observable behaviour, but it needs a mutable per-request cursor object (allocation and a place for bugs), it makes reentrancy and sub-pipeline mounting awkward because the cursor is shared state, and it hides the structure the fold makes obvious. Both are legitimate — Express uses a dispatcher, ASP.NET Core and Koa use the fold — and being able to compare them precisely is the senior answer. Deliberately ABSENT: any registry lookup at request time, any position awareness inside a middleware, and any inheritance hierarchy of middleware base classes."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "@FunctionalInterface interface Next { void run(); }\n@FunctionalInterface interface Handler { void handle(Context ctx); }\ninterface Middleware { void invoke(Context ctx, Next next); }\n\nfinal class Pipeline {\n  private final List<Middleware> mws = new ArrayList<>();\n  Pipeline use(Middleware m) { mws.add(m); return this; }   // registration order = outer to inner\n\n  Handler build(Handler terminal) {\n    Handler chain = terminal;                               // innermost first\n    for (int i = mws.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {             // FOLD back-to-front\n      final Middleware mw = mws.get(i);\n      final Handler inner = chain;                          // captured: everything below mw\n      chain = ctx -> mw.invoke(ctx, () -> inner.handle(ctx));\n    }\n    return chain;                                           // one callable, built once\n  }\n}\n\n// Runs BEFORE and AFTER the whole downstream stack — impossible with successor-pointer CoR.\nclass TimingMiddleware implements Middleware {\n  public void invoke(Context ctx, Next next) {\n    long t0 = System.nanoTime();\n    try { next.run(); }\n    finally { ctx.log(\"took \" + (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000 + \"ms\"); }\n  }\n}\n\n// Short-circuit: simply do not call next.\nclass AuthMiddleware implements Middleware {\n  private final TokenVerifier verifier;\n  AuthMiddleware(TokenVerifier v) { verifier = v; }\n  public void invoke(Context ctx, Next next) {\n    Principal p = verifier.verify(ctx.header(\"Authorization\"));\n    if (p == null) { ctx.respond(401, \"unauthorized\"); return; }   // rest never runs\n    ctx.put(Principal.class, p);\n    next.run();\n  }\n}\n\n// Error middleware: the onion means one try/catch covers everything registered below it.\nclass ErrorMiddleware implements Middleware {\n  public void invoke(Context ctx, Next next) {\n    try { next.run(); }\n    catch (HttpException e) { ctx.respond(e.status(), e.body()); }\n    catch (RuntimeException e) { ctx.log(\"unhandled\", e); ctx.respond(500, \"internal error\"); }\n  }\n}\n\n// Contract enforcement: wrap each registered middleware once, at build time.\nclass GuardedNext implements Next {\n  private final Next delegate; private final String owner;\n  private boolean called = false;\n  GuardedNext(Next d, String owner) { this.delegate = d; this.owner = owner; }\n  public void run() {\n    if (called) throw new IllegalStateException(owner + \" called next() twice\");\n    called = true;\n    delegate.run();\n  }\n  boolean wasCalled() { return called; }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the onion — why next is a continuation and the fold is the construction",
        "Say the model out loud before writing code: a middleware pipeline is an ONION, not a queue. A queue hands the request to component 1, then 2, then 3; each is finished when it passes the request along. An onion NESTS: middleware 1 contains middleware 2 contains middleware 3 contains the handler, and a request enters through every layer and exits back out through every layer in reverse. Everything hard about the design follows from choosing the second model, and the mechanism that encodes it is the signature invoke(ctx, next) where next is a CONTINUATION — a callable representing the entire remaining computation. Consider what that buys concretely. Timing: t0 before next.run(), delta after, in a finally so it records even when the stack below throws — the timing middleware measures everything below it because next.run() does not return until everything below it has finished. Transactions: begin before, commit after, rollback in catch. Response compression: call next first, then gzip whatever the downstream wrote. None of these are expressible if a middleware's only power is to forward to a successor and return. Now the CONSTRUCTION. The fold in build() walks the list backward and at each step produces a new Handler closure. Read the invariant: after processing index i, chain is a callable that runs middleware i and everything after it. Start with chain = terminal (the empty tail is correct), and the invariant is preserved at each step because the new closure calls mw.invoke with a Next that runs the old chain. When the loop ends, chain runs middleware 0 and everything below — the whole pipeline as ONE function. Three properties make this the elegant construction. It is done ONCE at startup, so per-request cost is exactly the function calls the semantics require, with no list indexing and no allocation. It is naturally reentrant and composable: a built pipeline IS a Handler, and a Handler is trivially adapted to a Middleware, so mounting a sub-pipeline under a path prefix is one line rather than a nested-cursor problem. And it makes the stack the data structure — a thread dump during a request literally shows the onion, outermost frame at the top, which is a debugging gift the index-walking dispatcher throws away by flattening every layer onto the same dispatch frame. The honest cost: with twenty middleware, the stack is twenty frames deeper, and a deeply recursive async-to-sync bridge can matter in a constrained runtime — but at framework scale this is noise compared to the clarity."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: contract violations — next twice, next never, and write-after-commit",
        "The pipeline's contract is tiny — call next at most once, do not write after the response is committed — and every real framework's bug tracker is full of violations, because the SYMPTOM always appears far from the CAUSE. Dissect all three. (1) CALLING NEXT TWICE: a middleware does next.run() inside a loop or on both branches of an if, and the entire downstream stack executes twice — the handler runs twice, so a POST creates two orders, and the response is written twice, producing corrupted framing or an IllegalStateException from a layer that had nothing to do with it. Detection is cheap and belongs in the framework, not in review: wrap each middleware's Next in a GuardedNext with a called flag that throws on the second invocation, naming the owning middleware. In production you keep the guard because its cost is one boolean and its value is an exact stack trace instead of a duplicated side effect. (2) NEVER CALLING NEXT: legitimate when deliberate (auth rejection, cache hit) and catastrophic when accidental — an early return on a branch the author forgot, and now requests silently vanish: no handler runs, no response is written, and the client hangs until timeout while the logs show nothing because nothing failed. Detection: after invoking the outermost handler, assert that either the response is committed or the terminal handler ran; the framework can also check per-middleware, since GuardedNext already knows whether it was called — a middleware that neither called next nor committed a response is a bug, and that is a precise, mechanically checkable rule. (3) WRITING AFTER COMMIT: the on-the-way-out half of the onion invites it — a middleware sets a header after next.run() returns, but the handler already flushed the response to the socket, so the header is silently dropped in some servers and throws in others. The fix is a state flag owned by the response object, flipped the instant the first byte is flushed, with every mutator checking it and throwing a message that names the field and the middleware. Encode the rule: everything that must appear on the wire must be set BEFORE next.run(); after next.run() you may only observe, unless the pipeline explicitly buffers the response (which is a deliberate, expensive choice — it defeats streaming and must not be the default). One design consequence ties the three together: because these guards need to know WHO violated the contract, wrap middleware at BUILD time with the registered name — the guard is part of the fold, costs nothing per request beyond an allocation the JIT usually elides, and converts three classes of spooky action at a distance into ordinary exceptions."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: short-circuiting, error propagation, and ordering as a design decision",
        "SHORT-CIRCUITING is the pipeline's most valuable property and it needs no mechanism at all — a middleware that does not call next has already short-circuited, and the layers ABOVE it still get their on-the-way-out code because their next.run() returns normally. That is exactly right: on a cache hit, the handler and the database layer are skipped but the timing and logging middleware still record the request, because they wrap the cache middleware. This is why ORDER is a design decision with teeth, and interviewers probe it: error handling and request-id assignment go outermost (they must see everything, including failures in auth); rate limiting goes above auth if you want to protect the token verifier from load, below it if your limits are per-user; caching goes below auth (never serve a cached private response to an unauthenticated caller — an ordering bug that is a security incident) but above the handler; body parsing and validation go closest to the handler so cheap rejections happen before expensive parsing. State the rule the ordering follows: cheap and universal outside, expensive and specific inside. ERROR PROPAGATION has two shapes and you should be able to argue both. The onion approach: errors are exceptions and propagate up the call stack through every next.run() that did not catch them, so an ErrorMiddleware registered outermost is one try/catch covering the whole application — and any middleware may catch a narrower error type at its own depth, which is a genuinely nice property (a retry middleware can catch a transient downstream failure and call next again, which is a legitimate second next call and the one case the double-call guard must be told about). The dedicated ERROR-MIDDLEWARE approach, familiar from Express: errors do not use the language's propagation at all; a middleware signals failure by passing the error into next(err) and the dispatcher then SKIPS forward to the next handler with an error-shaped signature. It exists because in a callback-based async runtime an exception thrown after the synchronous return cannot be caught by an enclosing try, so the framework has to rebuild propagation by hand. The trade-off is explicit: the onion gets propagation for free and keeps the stack trace intact but requires that the async model preserve the call relationship (async/await, coroutines, or synchronous threads); the error-channel approach works with raw callbacks but splits the middleware interface into two arities and loses stack context. In an async pipeline the whole design shifts one notch: Next returns a future, invoke returns a future, and the fold composes with flatMap instead of a plain call — the onion survives, but 'after next' now means 'in the continuation of the returned future', and forgetting that is the single most common async-middleware bug."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "FOLD vs INDEX-WALKING DISPATCHER: the fold builds once, allocates nothing per request, composes sub-pipelines for free, and puts the onion on the call stack where debuggers can see it; the dispatcher keeps a per-request cursor, which makes dynamic pipelines (adding a middleware mid-request, or a router that decides the tail after matching) easier to express and keeps the stack flat. Choose the fold for a fixed pipeline decided at startup — which is nearly always — and the dispatcher when the tail is genuinely dynamic. SYNCHRONOUS vs ASYNCHRONOUS Next: a void next.run() is simplest and correct on a thread-per-request server; a CompletableFuture-returning next is mandatory on an event loop but infects every middleware signature and makes 'after next' subtle. Do not offer both — a framework with two middleware interfaces is a framework where half the ecosystem is incompatible with the other half. EXCEPTION PROPAGATION vs ERROR CHANNEL: covered above; the deciding factor is whether your runtime preserves the call relationship across asynchrony. GLOBAL vs PER-ROUTE middleware: per-route is not a new concept, it is a sub-pipeline mounted as a middleware — resist adding a second registration mechanism. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE, in the LLD sense: the Context attribute bag is the design's soft spot — untyped keys make middleware coupling invisible, so use typed keys (a Key<T> class or ctx.put(Principal.class, p)) and document what each middleware publishes and requires; when a handler mysteriously reads null, the cause is almost always a middleware ordering change. Extensions worth naming: CONDITIONAL middleware (run only when a predicate matches — a wrapper middleware, not a framework feature), per-middleware TIMING and metrics (wrap each middleware at build time, the same hook the guards use), and STREAMING responses, which is the constraint that forbids buffer-everything error handling and forces the commit-flag discipline. The design's real proof of quality is that every one of these extensions is a new Middleware or a build-time wrapper, and none of them changes the two-method core."),
    ],
    "The middleware pipeline is the standard 'design a framework internal' question and it separates candidates instantly, because the naive answer — a list plus a for-loop — cannot express the three features every interviewer asks for next: measure the duration of everything downstream, catch an exception thrown anywhere below, and skip the rest on a cache hit. Getting to invoke(ctx, next) with next as a continuation, then building the chain by folding back-to-front so each middleware closes over the already-composed tail, demonstrates comfort with closures, higher-order composition, and Chain of Responsibility as it is actually used rather than as it appears in the pattern book. The follow-ups are reliably about the contract — what happens when next is called twice, never called, or when a header is set after the response committed — and about ordering (why cache must sit below auth), so a strong answer names the guards that detect each violation and states the ordering rule rather than listing middleware.",
    [
      G("successor_not_continuation", "Modelling middleware as classic Chain of Responsibility with a setNext successor pointer, where a component handles or forwards and control never returns.", "Pass the rest of the pipeline as a CONTINUATION — invoke(ctx, next) where next.run() executes everything downstream and returns — so a middleware has both a before and an after phase and can wrap the downstream call in try/finally.", "no way to express a timing or transaction middleware; duration measured inside the handler instead of around it; error handling duplicated in every component because nothing can wrap the stack below it."),
      G("index_walking_only", "Dispatching with a per-request index into the middleware list and no notion of composing the chain once, so sub-pipelines and mounted routers need special cases.", "Fold the list back-to-front at build time: chain = ctx -> mw.invoke(ctx, () -> inner.handle(ctx)) — the built pipeline is itself a Handler, so nesting is closed under composition and per-request cost is just the calls.", "a mutable cursor object allocated per request; middleware that needs to know its own position; router mounting implemented by splicing lists instead of nesting pipelines."),
      G("unguarded_next_contract", "Treating 'call next exactly once' as a convention, so a double call silently runs the handler twice and a forgotten call silently drops the request.", "Enforce it in the framework: wrap each middleware's Next at build time with a called-flag guard that throws (naming the middleware) on a second call, and assert after the outermost invocation that a response was committed or the terminal ran; guard writes with a committed flag on the response.", "duplicate side effects (two orders from one POST) with no local cause; requests hanging until client timeout with no error logged; headers set after next.run() silently missing from the wire."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.middleware, "Middleware pipeline class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── SERIALIZER / OBJECT MAPPER ───────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m18", "lld_m18_t8", 8, "Design a Serializer / Object Mapper", "design-serializer",
    ["case-study", "reflection", "security", "framework"],
    "Your object mapper handles Order and Customer fine. Then someone adds Order.customer and Customer.orders — a cycle — and the serializer blows the stack on a request that used to work. Then a field is typed Payment (abstract) with Card and UPI subclasses, and deserialization has no idea which one to build. Then a security review points out that the fix someone shipped for the second problem — reading the class name out of the JSON — is a remote code execution vector. Design the mapper so none of these three is an afterthought.",
    "One idea carries the whole design: the mapper never walks an object graph naively — it walks a CACHED FIELD PLAN with an IDENTITY SET in hand. Reflecting a class's fields is done once per type and memoized, so the per-object cost is a loop over a precomputed array rather than reflection. Cycles are broken by identity, not by depth limits: the first time an object is written it gets an $id, every later encounter writes {$ref}. And polymorphism is resolved by a type DISCRIMINATOR mapped through an explicit ALLOWLIST — mapping an arbitrary class name from untrusted input to a constructor is the single most reliably exploited bug class in every serialization library ever shipped.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: convert an arbitrary object graph to a wire format (JSON here, but the design must not be JSON-specific) and back. Serialization must handle primitives and boxed types, strings, enums, dates, collections and maps, nested user-defined objects, and null; it must support per-field customization — rename a field for the wire, omit a field, format a date, mask a secret — declared next to the field rather than in a giant central switch. Deserialization must construct instances (constructor or builder, not just field pokes into an already-allocated object), coerce types where safe, and be tolerant of UNKNOWN fields by default because producers and consumers of a payload are deployed at different times. Beyond the happy path, three hard requirements are the reason this question is asked: (1) the graph may contain CYCLES and shared references, and the mapper must not recurse forever; (2) a field's declared type may be abstract or an interface, so deserialization needs a way to pick the concrete type; (3) input is UNTRUSTED, so whatever mechanism answers (2) must not let the payload choose which class gets instantiated. Non-functional constraints that shape everything: throughput matters — serialization sits on every API response, so per-object reflection is unacceptable and metadata must be computed once per type and cached; payloads may be large enough that materializing the whole document in memory is not acceptable, so the design must be STREAMING at the token level with the tree model as an optional convenience; and the mapper must be thread-safe and shareable as a singleton, which forces all per-call state (the identity map, the depth counter) into a per-invocation context object. Explicitly out of scope: the byte-level JSON grammar, and schema evolution policy beyond field-level tolerance."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "OBJECTMAPPER is the facade: writeValue(Object) and readValue(String, Class<T>). It holds no per-call state; it owns the registry and the type-descriptor cache and is safe to share. TYPEDESCRIPTOR is the memoized field plan for one class: an array of FIELDACCESSOR entries (field name on the wire, the reflective accessor or generated getter, the declared type, and the per-field options — omit-if-null, format, alias), plus the chosen instantiation strategy (canonical constructor, builder, or no-arg constructor plus setters). It is computed on first sight of a class and cached forever in a ConcurrentHashMap keyed by Class, which is the single most important performance decision in the design. CODEC<T> (write(JsonGenerator, T, Ctx) and read(JsonParser, Ctx)) is the Strategy for one type; built-ins exist for primitives, collections, maps, dates and enums, and a generic ReflectiveCodec handles user types by walking a TypeDescriptor. SERIALIZERREGISTRY maps Class to Codec, resolving by exact type then by supertype, and is the extension point where an application registers a custom codec for Money or a legacy date format. JSONGENERATOR and JSONPARSER are the streaming boundary — writeFieldName, writeNumber, nextToken — and keeping the codecs on top of a token stream rather than a tree is what makes large payloads viable. SERIALIZATIONCONTEXT is the per-call state: the IDENTITYTRACKER (an IdentityHashMap from object to assigned id), the current depth, and the active view/profile. TYPERESOLVER handles polymorphism: on write it emits the discriminator value for a concrete class; on read it maps a discriminator value to a class THROUGH AN ALLOWLIST, and this is the security-critical object in the whole design."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The mapper is a STRATEGY registry (Codec per type) driven by a two-phase pipeline: resolve the codec for the runtime type, then let it emit tokens, recursing through the registry for nested values. Two design decisions deserve argument. FIRST, HOW FIELDS ARE MAPPED. Option A is RUNTIME REFLECTION plus annotations: at first encounter, read the class's declared fields and annotations, build the TypeDescriptor, cache it. Option B is COMPILE-TIME CODEGEN: an annotation processor generates a hand-written-looking codec per class at build time. The trade-off is concrete and worth stating precisely — reflection costs a slow first call per type (class scanning, accessor setup, setAccessible) and defers all errors to runtime, so a field whose type has no codec fails on the first request rather than at build; it also keeps the library completely decoupled from the build system, which is why it dominates in practice. Codegen has zero startup cost, is friendly to ahead-of-time compilation and to native images where reflection metadata must be declared explicitly, and turns 'no codec for this type' into a COMPILE ERROR — real type safety — at the price of build complexity and generated code the developer must be able to read when it misbehaves. The pragmatic middle, which is what mature libraries actually do, is reflection ONCE into a cached plan plus a fast-path accessor (MethodHandle or generated LambdaMetafactory accessors), which recovers most of the codegen throughput without the build-time coupling. SECOND, INSTANTIATION. Prefer a canonical constructor or builder over allocate-then-set: it lets the target type keep its invariants and stay immutable, which matters because a mapper that requires a no-arg constructor and mutable fields forces every DTO in the codebase to be a mutable bag. Patterns present: STRATEGY (Codec), FLYWEIGHT-ish caching (TypeDescriptor), VISITOR-flavoured traversal (the codec walks the plan and emits into a generator), and a small BUILDER for mapper configuration. Deliberately absent: any inheritance requirement on user classes — a mapper that demands 'implements Serializable' is a mapper that cannot serialize types you do not own."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class ObjectMapper {\n  private final Map<Class<?>, TypeDescriptor> plans = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  private final SerializerRegistry registry;\n  private final TypeResolver typeResolver;   // discriminator <-> ALLOWLISTED class\n\n  String writeValue(Object root) {\n    JsonGenerator gen = new JsonGenerator();\n    write(root, gen, new Ctx());\n    return gen.toString();\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  void write(Object v, JsonGenerator gen, Ctx ctx) {\n    if (v == null) { gen.writeNull(); return; }\n    Codec<Object> c = (Codec<Object>) registry.codecFor(v.getClass());\n    if (c != null) { c.write(gen, v, ctx); return; }        // primitives, collections, custom\n    writeBean(v, gen, ctx);\n  }\n\n  private void writeBean(Object v, JsonGenerator gen, Ctx ctx) {\n    Integer seen = ctx.ids.get(v);                          // IdentityHashMap: reference equality\n    if (seen != null) { gen.writeStartObject(); gen.writeField(\"$ref\", seen); gen.writeEndObject(); return; }\n    int id = ctx.nextId();\n    ctx.ids.put(v, id);                                     // register BEFORE recursing\n    if (ctx.depth++ > ctx.maxDepth) throw new MappingException(\"max depth exceeded\");\n    gen.writeStartObject();\n    gen.writeField(\"$id\", id);\n    String disc = typeResolver.discriminatorFor(v.getClass());\n    if (disc != null) gen.writeField(\"@type\", disc);         // polymorphic marker\n    for (FieldAccessor f : plan(v.getClass()).fields()) {\n      Object fv = f.get(v);\n      if (fv == null && f.omitIfNull()) continue;\n      gen.writeFieldName(f.wireName());\n      write(fv, gen, ctx);\n    }\n    gen.writeEndObject();\n    ctx.depth--;\n  }\n\n  private TypeDescriptor plan(Class<?> c) {\n    return plans.computeIfAbsent(c, TypeDescriptor::reflect);   // reflect ONCE per class\n  }\n}\n\n// The security boundary: a discriminator is a KEY into a registered map, never a class name.\nfinal class TypeResolver {\n  private final Map<String, Class<?>> byName = new HashMap<>();   // allowlist\n  private final Map<Class<?>, String> byClass = new HashMap<>();\n  void register(String discriminator, Class<?> type) {\n    byName.put(discriminator, type); byClass.put(type, discriminator);\n  }\n  String discriminatorFor(Class<?> c) { return byClass.get(c); }\n  Class<?> resolve(String discriminator) {\n    Class<?> c = byName.get(discriminator);\n    if (c == null) throw new MappingException(\"unknown type id: \" + discriminator);\n    return c;                                    // NEVER Class.forName(fromPayload)\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class Ctx {\n  final Map<Object, Integer> ids = new IdentityHashMap<>();\n  int depth = 0; final int maxDepth = 200;\n  private int seq = 0;\n  int nextId() { return ++seq; }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the field-mapping strategy — reflection with annotations vs compile-time codegen",
        "Everything the mapper does per object reduces to 'for each field, get the value and emit it', so the design question is where the list of fields and their wire options comes from. The naive implementation asks reflection EVERY time: getDeclaredFields on each call, getAnnotation on each field, setAccessible, then Field.get. This is correct and catastrophically slow — field lookup allocates a fresh array on each getDeclaredFields call, annotation lookup parses metadata, and the whole thing sits on every API response. The first and largest win is memoization: compute a TYPEDESCRIPTOR once per class and cache it in a ConcurrentHashMap keyed by Class. Now the per-object cost is a loop over a plain array of FieldAccessor objects, and reflection appears exactly once in the process's lifetime per type. The second win, if profiling demands it, is replacing Field.get with a MethodHandle or a LambdaMetafactory-generated Function so the JIT can inline the access; this closes most of the gap to hand-written code. COMPILE-TIME CODEGEN takes the idea to its conclusion: an annotation processor emits, at build time, a codec class per user type containing literal gen.writeFieldName(\"total\"); gen.writeNumber(o.total()); calls. Compare honestly along four axes. STARTUP: reflection pays a first-call penalty per type — invisible in a long-lived server, painful in a serverless function or a CLI where every invocation is a cold start; codegen pays nothing. TYPE SAFETY AND ERROR TIMING: codegen fails the BUILD when a field has no codec or an annotation is misconfigured; reflection fails the first production request that touches that field, which is the difference between a red CI job and a 500 at 3am. AOT AND NATIVE IMAGES: reflective access must be declared in configuration for a native image; generated codecs work unchanged, which is why the codegen approach has gained ground. FLEXIBILITY AND ECOSYSTEM: reflection can serialize third-party types you cannot annotate, supports runtime-configured views and mix-ins, and needs no build plugin — which is precisely why the reflective mappers won the ecosystem despite being slower. The senior answer does not pick a winner in the abstract; it says: reflect once into a cached plan by default, expose a codec registry so hot types can be hand-written, and offer codegen where cold start or AOT is the binding constraint. It also names the shared invariant both approaches must satisfy — the plan is IMMUTABLE and computed before any concurrent use, because a mutable per-type plan is a data race in a mapper that is deliberately shared as a singleton."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: cyclic references — the infinite recursion, and the two real fixes",
        "Add one back-reference to a domain model and the naive serializer dies. Order has a Customer; someone adds Customer.orders for a convenience query; now writing an Order writes its Customer, which writes its orders, which writes the same Order, forever — a StackOverflowError on a code path that has nothing to do with the change, produced by a data model most ORMs generate by default. The instinct to reach for a DEPTH LIMIT is wrong and worth rejecting explicitly: it converts a crash into truncated data, the cut point depends on traversal order, and a legitimately deep tree gets mangled while the cycle is still there. There are exactly two principled fixes. FIX ONE, IDENTITY-BASED REFERENCE TRACKING: keep a per-invocation IdentityHashMap from object to an assigned integer id. The first time an object is written, assign an id, emit it as $id, and write the fields normally; every subsequent encounter with the SAME instance emits {\"$ref\": id} instead of recursing. Three details are load-bearing. The map must use REFERENCE equality (IdentityHashMap, not HashMap) — two equal-but-distinct value objects are legitimately separate nodes, and hashing user-defined equals on a partially-built graph is both wrong and a performance trap. The id must be registered BEFORE recursing into the fields, or a self-referencing object still recurses once too far. And deserialization must be symmetric: a two-pass read that records $id targets and patches $ref placeholders afterwards, because a $ref can legally point at an object whose parse has not finished — which is the wrinkle that makes reference tracking genuinely harder than it looks. The payoff is that the graph round-trips exactly, shared instances stay shared, and no data is lost. The cost is a non-standard wire format ($id/$ref are a convention, not JSON), so any consumer that is not your mapper sees noise. FIX TWO, EXPLICIT BACK-REFERENCE EXCLUSION: declare which side of the relationship is the 'parent' and simply do not serialize it — annotate Customer.orders as the managed side and Order.customer as the back-reference that is omitted on write and re-attached on read from the enclosing context. This produces CLEAN, conventional JSON that any consumer can read, and it is the right answer for public APIs. Its cost is that the omitted edge is genuinely absent from the payload, so a consumer who needs it must reconstruct it, and identity of shared non-parent objects is lost (the same Address referenced by two Orders is emitted twice and becomes two objects on read). The design decision follows from the audience: internal graph transport that must round-trip exactly gets $id/$ref; a public API contract gets exclusion, usually via a dedicated DTO that has no cycle to begin with — and the observation that the cleanest fix is often 'do not serialize the domain model' is worth saying out loud. Whichever is chosen, keep the depth counter as a BACKSTOP against a hostile or malformed graph, not as the cycle strategy."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: polymorphic deserialization, the RCE vector, and version tolerance",
        "Serialization of a polymorphic field is easy — you have the runtime object, so write its concrete fields. DESERIALIZATION is the hard direction: the field is declared Payment, the payload is a JSON object, and nothing in the type system says whether to construct Card or UPI. The mechanism is a TYPE DISCRIMINATOR: a property in the payload (\"@type\": \"card\") or an enclosing wrapper that names the concrete shape, written on the way out by the same resolver that reads it on the way in, so the round-trip is symmetric by construction. The design question is what the discriminator is ALLOWED to be, and this is where a design interview turns into a security interview. The dangerous implementation writes the fully-qualified class name and, on read, calls Class.forName(nameFromPayload) and instantiates it. This is the classic UNSAFE DESERIALIZATION vulnerability, and the reason it is a REMOTE CODE EXECUTION vector rather than merely sloppy is worth being able to explain: the attacker does not need a class that does anything obviously malicious, only a class on your classpath — from any transitive dependency — whose construction, property setters, or eventual finalization triggers a side effect. Chain a few such GADGETS together (a map whose lookup invokes a comparator, a comparator that invokes a template engine, a template that executes an expression) and constructing an object is enough to run a command. The attacker controls one string in a JSON body; the vulnerable code turns it into 'instantiate anything on the classpath'. Every mapper that shipped this feature — including the big ones — has a long CVE trail of gadget chains, and the fixes that tried to keep the feature safe by BLOCKLISTING known gadget classes failed repeatedly because the next dependency ships the next gadget. The correct design is an ALLOWLIST: the discriminator is a KEY into a registry the application populated at startup with the specific subtypes it intends to accept; an unknown key is a validation error, not a lookup. Note the secondary benefit — the wire format now carries \"card\", not \"com.acme.billing.internal.CardPayment\", so refactoring a package does not break every stored document. Two related concerns finish the topic. VERSION TOLERANCE: producers and consumers deploy at different times, so an unknown field must NOT be a hard failure by default — ignore it, optionally with a metric, and offer strict mode for internal contracts where a typo should fail loudly; a missing field maps to null or a declared default. The symmetric rule is that the mapper should never silently drop data it received on a round-trip if the payload is meant to be re-emitted (an 'any-setter' bag preserves it), which is the difference between a mapper used in a proxy and one used at the edge. STREAMING: a large payload must not be materialized as a tree — the codec API is written against a token generator and a pull parser precisely so a 200MB array can be read element by element with bounded memory, and any convenience tree model is built on top of that, never underneath it. State the limits explicitly too: a maximum depth, a maximum document size, and a maximum collection size, because a mapper that will happily allocate whatever an untrusted payload declares is a denial-of-service primitive."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "REFLECTION vs CODEGEN: reflection into a cached plan wins on flexibility, third-party types, runtime configuration, and zero build coupling, and its per-object cost after caching is close to codegen; codegen wins on cold start, AOT/native images, and moving 'no codec for this type' from a runtime 500 into a compile error. Pick by deployment shape — a long-lived server barely notices the first-call penalty, a serverless function does. $ID/$REF vs BACK-REFERENCE EXCLUSION for cycles: reference tracking round-trips the graph exactly and preserves shared identity but emits a non-standard format only your mapper understands; exclusion produces clean, public-API-grade JSON at the cost of losing the omitted edge and de-duplication. For external contracts, the third option usually wins outright: serialize a purpose-built DTO with no cycle, which is less machinery and a better contract. DISCRIMINATOR STYLE: an inline property (\"@type\") is compact and streaming-friendly but pollutes the object's namespace and can collide with a real field; a wrapper ({\"card\": {...}}) is unambiguous and easy to validate against a schema but changes the shape and is more awkward to read incrementally. Both are fine; class-name-based resolution is not, at any price. TOLERANT vs STRICT unknown fields: tolerant by default is required for independent deploys; strict is right for internal service-to-service contracts where a misspelled field should fail in staging rather than silently default to null — make it a per-mapper setting, not a global one. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: the TypeDescriptor cache is keyed by Class and therefore pins classloaders — in a container that hot-reloads applications this is a classic leak, and the fix is a weak-keyed cache; generics erase, so a plan must capture the resolved generic type of each field (a TypeReference) or List<Order> deserializes into List<LinkedHashMap>, the single most common object-mapper bug in the wild. Extension points to name: a codec registry for value types (Money, a legacy date format), per-field converters declared by annotation, VIEWS or projections that select a subset of fields per audience (the plan already has the field list, so a view is a filter over it, not a second traversal), and a pluggable wire format — because the codecs are written against a token generator, swapping JSON for a binary format is a new generator implementation and no change to any codec."),
    ],
    "The object mapper is a favourite senior-LLD problem because it starts easy and every follow-up is a trap the candidate either knows or does not. The first is performance — anyone who reflects per object rather than caching a field plan per class has not built one. The second is cycles, where the tell is reaching for a depth limit instead of identity-based $id/$ref tracking or explicit back-reference exclusion, and where the strong candidate also notes that the real fix for a public API is a DTO with no cycle. The third is polymorphic deserialization, which doubles as a security screen: proposing that the payload name a class and the mapper call Class.forName is an instant red flag, and being able to explain WHY gadget chains make it remote code execution — and why blocklists failed while allowlists work — is exactly the depth that separates a senior from a mid. Version tolerance for unknown fields, streaming for large payloads, and the erased-generics trap round out the follow-ups.",
    [
      G("reflect_per_object", "Calling getDeclaredFields, reading annotations, and doing setAccessible on every serialize call instead of once per class.", "Compute an immutable TypeDescriptor field plan on first sight of a class and memoize it in a ConcurrentHashMap keyed by Class; the per-object path becomes a loop over a precomputed array, with MethodHandle or generated accessors if profiling demands more.", "reflection calls inside the per-object write loop; serialization throughput that scales with field count and class complexity rather than data size; no cache keyed by Class anywhere in the design."),
      G("depth_limit_for_cycles", "Handling a cyclic object graph by capping recursion depth, producing truncated output whose cut point depends on traversal order.", "Break cycles by IDENTITY: an IdentityHashMap assigns an $id on first write (registered before recursing) and emits {$ref} on every later encounter, with a two-pass read that patches placeholders — or exclude the back-reference explicitly for clean public-API JSON. Keep the depth cap only as a hostile-input backstop.", "StackOverflowError on graphs with bidirectional associations; a maxDepth constant used as the cycle strategy; a plain HashMap (value equality) used for the seen-set instead of IdentityHashMap."),
      G("classname_type_resolution", "Resolving polymorphic types by writing the fully-qualified class name into the payload and calling Class.forName on whatever comes back.", "Use a short discriminator that is a KEY into an application-registered ALLOWLIST of accepted subtypes; an unknown key is a validation error. Never let untrusted input select a class — gadget chains turn instantiation alone into remote code execution, and blocklists have failed repeatedly where allowlists hold.", "Class.forName or newInstance applied to a string taken from the request body; a deny-list of known dangerous classes; wire formats containing internal package names, which also breaks every stored document on a refactor."),
    ],
    0.6, DIAG.serializer, "Serializer / object mapper class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── B+TREE (DATABASE INDEX) ──────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m19", "lld_m19_t1", 1, "Design a B+Tree (Database Index)", "design-bplus-tree",
    ["case-study", "index", "disk", "data-structure"],
    "A balanced binary search tree gives O(log n) lookups, which sounds optimal — yet no database on earth indexes with one. With 100 million rows a red-black tree is about 27 levels deep, and if each level is a different disk page that is 27 I/Os for one lookup. What does a B+Tree change to turn that into three, and why does the answer also make range scans nearly free? Design the index.",
    "Stop counting comparisons and start counting I/Os — that single reframing is the whole design. A node is sized to exactly one disk PAGE, so instead of one key and two children per node you get hundreds of keys and hundreds of children, and the tree's height collapses to log base 400 of n: three or four levels for hundreds of millions of keys, three or four page reads for a lookup, with the top levels permanently cached. The B+ variant adds the second half: internal nodes hold only routing keys and ALL values live in the leaves, which are chained in a linked list — so a range scan is one descent followed by a sequential walk, never a repeated root-to-leaf traversal.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: an ordered index supporting get(key) returning the associated value or record pointer, put(key, value) with replace-on-duplicate semantics (or an explicit duplicate policy), delete(key), and — the operation that motivates the whole shape — rangeScan(lo, hi) returning every entry in KEY ORDER, plus its cousins scanFrom(k) and full ordered iteration. Keys are comparable; the index must stay balanced under any insertion order, including the extremely common ascending pattern of an auto-increment primary key. Non-functional constraints are the design, not decoration. First, the data does not fit in memory, so the index lives on disk (or on a device where access is block-granular, which includes SSDs) and the cost model is NUMBER OF PAGE READS, not number of comparisons — comparisons within a page that is already in memory are effectively free. Second, a page is a fixed size (commonly 4KB to 16KB) and is read and written atomically as a unit, so the node must be designed to fill a page and any node smaller than a page wastes the read. Third, space must be used reasonably — an index that is half empty doubles the pages that must be read for a scan — which produces the OCCUPANCY INVARIANT: every node except the root is at least half full. Fourth, many readers and writers hit the index concurrently, so the design must say how a descending traversal is made safe without holding a lock on the root for the duration of every operation, which would serialize the entire database. Explicitly in scope for the LLD framing: the node API, the invariants, and the split/merge contracts. Explicitly out of scope: the buffer pool's replacement policy, the write-ahead log, and MVCC — but the design must name where it hands off to each."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "NODE is the abstract base and the unit of I/O: it carries a sorted key array, a key count, and a page id; its invariant is that keys are sorted and that count is between ceil(m/2)-1 and m-1 for a non-root node, where m is the branching factor. INTERNALNODE holds n keys and n+1 CHILD POINTERS (page ids, not object references — an important modelling detail, since the child may not be in memory), and its keys are pure ROUTING SEPARATORS: key[i] means 'everything at or above this goes right'. It stores no values at all, which is the defining B+ property and is what lets it pack more keys per page than a B-Tree node of the same size. LEAFNODE holds n keys and n VALUES (or record pointers), plus a NEXT pointer to its right sibling — the linked list that makes range scans sequential — and often a PREV pointer for backward scans. All leaves are at the same depth, which is the balance invariant, and it is maintained not by rotations but by growth at the ROOT: the tree gets taller only when the root splits, so every leaf gains a level simultaneously. SPLITRESULT is the small value object that carries a split back up the recursion: the promoted separator key and the new right node. PAGESTORE / BUFFERPOOL is the collaborator that turns a page id into an in-memory node and back, and it is what the design leans on rather than reimplements: fetch(pageId), allocate(), and markDirty(node). BPLUSTREE is the facade owning the root page id and sequencing descend, mutate, and rebalance. LATCH is the short-duration physical lock on a single page — deliberately distinct from a transactional LOCK on a key — and the distinction is worth stating because conflating the two is how candidates end up proposing that a lookup holds the root for the length of a transaction."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Design the node API around the two questions the tree asks of it: WHERE does this key belong (a binary search over the sorted key array returning an insertion point) and DO I HAVE ROOM. Everything else — split, merge, redistribute — is expressed in terms of those two plus the occupancy invariant, which keeps the tree class small and the node class honest. The traversal is a single recursive or iterative descent from the root: at each internal node, binary search the separators to pick a child page id, fetch it through the buffer pool, and repeat until a leaf. Note what that means for the object model — the tree never holds a Java reference to a child; it holds a PAGE ID and asks the store, which is the seam that makes the same structure work in memory and on disk and is the modelling point interviewers look for. INSERT is descend-then-unwind: find the leaf, insert in sorted position, and if the leaf overflows, split it and return a SplitResult to the parent, which inserts the promoted key and may itself split, recursively; if the ROOT splits, allocate a new root with two children and the tree grows by one level. DELETE is the mirror: remove from the leaf, and if occupancy drops below half, first try to REDISTRIBUTE from a sibling that can spare a key (cheap, local, no structural change), and only merge with a sibling when neither can spare one, which propagates a key removal to the parent and may collapse the root. The asymmetry is deliberate — redistribution is preferred because a merge is a structural change that must be logged and that may cascade. Patterns present, and honestly labelled: this is not a pattern-heavy design, it is an invariant-heavy one, and saying that is better than manufacturing a Visitor. The composite-ish Node/Internal/Leaf split is real polymorphism (descend and insert differ), the SplitResult is a small value object that keeps the recursion clean instead of using out-parameters, and the PageStore is a straightforward Repository boundary. Deliberately absent: rotations (a B+Tree rebalances by split/merge, not rotation), and any per-node lock held for the duration of an operation."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "abstract class Node {\n  long pageId;\n  int count;                       // keys in use\n  final Key[] keys;                // sorted\n  Node(int order) { keys = new Key[order]; }\n  boolean isFull(int order)  { return count == order - 1; }\n  boolean isUnderflow(int order) { return count < (order + 1) / 2 - 1; }\n  int search(Key k) {              // binary search -> insertion point\n    int lo = 0, hi = count;\n    while (lo < hi) { int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1; if (keys[mid].compareTo(k) < 0) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid; }\n    return lo;\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class InternalNode extends Node {   // routing only: NO values\n  final long[] children;                  // count + 1 child page ids\n  InternalNode(int order) { super(order); children = new long[order + 1]; }\n}\n\nfinal class LeafNode extends Node {       // ALL values live here\n  final Value[] values;\n  long next = -1, prev = -1;              // sibling chain -> sequential range scans\n  LeafNode(int order) { super(order); values = new Value[order]; }\n}\n\nfinal class BPlusTree {\n  private final int order;                // branching factor: chosen so a node fills one page\n  private final PageStore store;\n  private long rootId;\n\n  Value get(Key k) {\n    Node n = store.fetch(rootId);\n    while (n instanceof InternalNode in) n = store.fetch(in.children[in.search(k)]);   // ~height I/Os\n    LeafNode leaf = (LeafNode) n;\n    int i = leaf.search(k);\n    return (i < leaf.count && leaf.keys[i].equals(k)) ? leaf.values[i] : null;\n  }\n\n  List<Value> rangeScan(Key lo, Key hi) {\n    Node n = store.fetch(rootId);\n    while (n instanceof InternalNode in) n = store.fetch(in.children[in.search(lo)]);\n    List<Value> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    LeafNode leaf = (LeafNode) n;\n    int i = leaf.search(lo);\n    while (leaf != null) {                       // ONE descent, then a sequential walk\n      for (; i < leaf.count; i++) {\n        if (leaf.keys[i].compareTo(hi) > 0) return out;\n        out.add(leaf.values[i]);\n      }\n      leaf = leaf.next < 0 ? null : (LeafNode) store.fetch(leaf.next);\n      i = 0;\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n\n  void put(Key k, Value v) {\n    SplitResult s = insert(store.fetch(rootId), k, v);\n    if (s == null) return;\n    InternalNode newRoot = new InternalNode(order);   // tree grows ONLY at the root\n    newRoot.pageId = store.allocate();\n    newRoot.keys[0] = s.promotedKey; newRoot.count = 1;\n    newRoot.children[0] = rootId; newRoot.children[1] = s.rightPageId;\n    store.markDirty(newRoot);\n    rootId = newRoot.pageId;                          // all leaves gain a level at once\n  }\n\n  // Leaf split: the median key is COPIED up (it still exists in the right leaf).\n  // Internal split: the median key is MOVED up (routing keys are not data).\n  private SplitResult insert(Node n, Key k, Value v) { /* descend, insert, split on overflow */ return null; }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the I/O-count argument — why not a binary tree, and how height 3 happens",
        "The question 'why do databases use B+Trees rather than balanced binary trees' has exactly one correct answer and it is not 'they are faster' — both are logarithmic. It is that the cost model is wrong. A balanced BST is optimal when the unit of work is a COMPARISON; a disk-resident index's unit of work is a PAGE READ, and a page read costs roughly 100 microseconds on a spinning disk and tens of microseconds on an SSD, versus nanoseconds for an in-memory comparison. Run the numbers, because the interviewer wants the numbers. With 100 million keys, a red-black tree has height about log2(10^8), roughly 27; every node is a small heap object landing on an arbitrary page, so a lookup is up to 27 page reads and about 27 cache misses even in memory. Now build a node that fills a 16KB page: with a 16-byte key and an 8-byte child pointer, a node holds roughly 16384/24, call it about 680 entries, so the branching factor m is in the high hundreds. Height is log base m of n: log base 680 of 10^8 is under 3. That is THREE page reads instead of twenty-seven, and it gets better — the root is always in the buffer pool, and the entire second level of a three-level tree is a few hundred pages, so it is cached too. The real cost of a lookup on a warm index is ONE page read, the leaf. The general form worth stating: height is ceil(log_m(n)) and m is set by the page size divided by the entry size, so doubling the page size or halving the key size buys you fanout, and fanout is the only lever on height. Two corollaries follow, and mentioning them shows you understand the mechanism rather than the fact. First, this is exactly why the B+ variant packs internal nodes with routing keys ONLY: every byte of value stored in an internal node is a byte not spent on a child pointer, so moving values to the leaves directly increases m and therefore directly reduces height. A B-Tree that stores values in internal nodes has lower fanout for the same page size and is measurably taller. Second, the same argument applies one level up the memory hierarchy: an in-memory B-Tree with nodes sized to a CACHE LINE beats a binary tree for the identical reason with 'page read' replaced by 'cache miss', which is why cache-conscious B-Trees appear in in-memory databases. And it explains the design's most counter-intuitive property: a B+Tree does MORE comparisons than a binary tree (a binary search within each fat node, plus the descent) and is dramatically faster, because comparisons inside a resident page are free relative to fetching the next page. If you can articulate that inversion, you have answered the question the topic exists to ask."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: splits, merges, and the half-full invariant that keeps the tree balanced",
        "Balance in a B+Tree is not maintained by rotations; it is a consequence of two rules — every node except the root holds between ceil(m/2)-1 and m-1 keys, and all leaves are at the same depth — enforced by split on overflow and merge/redistribute on underflow. INSERT: descend to the target leaf, insert in sorted position, and if that pushes the leaf to m keys, SPLIT it: allocate a new right leaf, move the upper half of the entries into it, fix the next/prev chain, and hand the parent a promoted separator. The detail that separates a candidate who has implemented one from a candidate who has read about one is the difference between the two splits. A LEAF split COPIES the median key up: the key still lives in the right leaf, because leaves hold the actual data and losing the key would lose the row. An INTERNAL split MOVES the median key up: routing keys are not data, the promoted key's job is now done by its new position in the parent, and leaving a copy behind would break the search invariant. Get that backwards and lookups silently miss one key per split. The parent may now overflow and split in turn, and so on up; if the ROOT splits, a new root is allocated with two children and the height increases by one — the only way the tree ever grows taller, and the reason all leaves stay at equal depth without any explicit rebalancing. Note the amortized cost: a split touches O(height) pages in the worst case, but splits are rare (one per m/2 insertions into a given leaf, roughly), so the amortized page writes per insert is small; the worst case per operation is still O(height) writes and that matters for latency tails. DELETE is the mirror and is where implementations most often cheat. Remove the entry from the leaf; if the leaf still holds at least ceil(m/2)-1 keys, done. Otherwise try REDISTRIBUTION (also called borrowing) first: if an adjacent sibling with the same parent has more than the minimum, move one entry across and UPDATE THE PARENT'S SEPARATOR to the new boundary — cheap, local, no structural change, no cascade. Only when neither sibling can spare a key do you MERGE: concatenate the node with a sibling (their combined size is guaranteed to fit, since both are at or below minimum), remove the separator from the parent, and fix the leaf chain. The merge may push the parent into underflow, cascading upward; if the root ends up with a single child, delete the root and make that child the new root — the only way the tree gets shorter. Preferring redistribution to merging is a deliberate design decision worth defending: merges are structural, must be logged, and can cascade to the root, so doing the local thing when possible keeps the common case cheap. One honest production footnote: many real engines relax deletion, tolerating under-full nodes and reclaiming space during vacuum or rebuild rather than merging eagerly, because merges under concurrency are the hardest operation in the structure to make safe. Saying that — and saying WHY — is senior signal."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: concurrency — latch crabbing, and why sequential keys are the pathological case",
        "The naive concurrency answer, one lock over the whole tree, is correct and useless: every lookup in the database serializes on the root. The real technique is LATCH CRABBING (also called lock coupling), and its name describes the motion. Descending for a READ: latch the root in shared mode, find the child, latch the child in shared mode, and only THEN release the parent's latch — you always hold two latches for an instant, hand over hand, like a crab moving along a rope. No thread ever holds a latch on the root for more than the time it takes to binary-search one page, so the root stops being a global bottleneck. For a WRITE the same motion runs with exclusive latches plus one extra rule: after latching a child exclusively, check whether that child is SAFE — for an insert, safe means not full, so a split cannot propagate past it; for a delete, safe means above the minimum, so a merge cannot propagate past it. If the child is safe, you may release ALL ancestor latches immediately, because whatever happens below cannot touch them. If it is not safe, you must keep the ancestors latched, since the split or merge may propagate up. The practical consequence is that most writes release the root almost immediately and only the rare structure-modifying write holds a path. The standard refinement is OPTIMISTIC crabbing: assume the write will not split, descend with shared latches taking only the leaf exclusively, and if the leaf turns out to be full, abandon the descent and retry pessimistically from the root — the fast path is then as cheap as a read. Two distinctions must be kept crisp because interviewers probe them. LATCHES vs LOCKS: a latch is a short physical guard on a page held for microseconds and released before the operation returns; a transactional lock is on a KEY or a range and is held until commit. Confusing them produces the classic wrong answer 'hold the leaf latch until the transaction commits', which would block the entire page for the transaction's lifetime. And LATCH ORDERING: crabbing always moves top-down, and structure modifications must never latch upward while holding a lower latch, or two concurrent splits deadlock — real implementations either restart the operation from the root under exclusive latches or use a B-link tree, where each node carries a right-sibling pointer and a high key so a reader that arrives after a split can simply follow the link rightward instead of being wrong, which lets splits happen without latching the parent at all. Finally, the pathological workload worth naming: MONOTONICALLY INCREASING keys, the auto-increment primary key. Every insert lands in the rightmost leaf, so that one page is the exclusive-latch hot spot for the whole table, and a naive even split leaves every left-hand leaf permanently half full — 50% space utilization across the index. Real engines special-case it with a RIGHTMOST SPLIT that puts almost everything in the left node and starts the new right node nearly empty, restoring near-100% fill; the contention on the rightmost leaf remains, which is one of the honest reasons write-heavy systems reach for the LSM-Tree instead."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "B+TREE vs B-TREE: storing values in internal nodes (a plain B-Tree) can answer a lucky lookup one level early, but it lowers fanout for the same page size, raises height for everyone, and — decisively — destroys cheap range scans, since an ordered traversal must walk internal nodes rather than a leaf chain. B+ wins for a database index and the reasoning is fanout plus scans, not elegance. B+TREE vs HASH INDEX: a hash index is O(1) expected for exact-match lookups and beats a B+Tree on that one operation, but supports no ordering — no range, no prefix, no ORDER BY without a sort, no min/max shortcut — which is why it is offered as a secondary option and never as the default. B+TREE vs LSM-TREE: the B+Tree updates IN PLACE, giving low read amplification (about height page reads, usually one warm) at the cost of random writes and a hot rightmost page under sequential inserts; the LSM-Tree buffers writes in memory and flushes immutable sorted runs, giving excellent write throughput at the cost of read amplification and background compaction. Read-heavy and range-scan-heavy OLTP takes the B+Tree; write-heavy ingestion takes the LSM-Tree — and knowing that the choice is a workload question, not a quality question, is the point. B+TREE vs SKIP LIST: comparable asymptotics and much simpler concurrent implementations, which is why skip lists appear in MEMORY (including inside LSM memtables), but a skip list's node-per-entry layout is hostile to block devices, so it does not replace the on-disk index. Extension points and what breaks at scale: PREFIX COMPRESSION of routing keys raises fanout further and is nearly free; a FILL FACTOR below 100% at build time leaves room for later inserts and reduces split churn; VARIABLE-LENGTH keys break the neat fixed-order arithmetic and force a slotted-page layout where 'full' is measured in bytes rather than entries — a modelling detail worth mentioning, since it changes isFull() from a count check to a space check. COVERING and COMPOSITE indexes are just a wider leaf entry, which is why an index that carries the queried columns avoids the table lookup entirely. And FRAGMENTATION after heavy delete-and-insert cycles degrades scan locality even while the tree stays technically balanced, which is why production databases periodically REBUILD an index rather than trusting incremental rebalancing forever."),
    ],
    "The B+Tree is the canonical 'do you understand the storage layer' question, and it is graded almost entirely on one thing: whether you reason in PAGE READS rather than comparisons. The strong answer opens by rejecting the binary tree with numbers — 27 levels versus 3 for 100 million keys — derives the branching factor from the page size divided by the entry size, and then explains that the B+ variant moves all values to the leaves precisely to buy more fanout, with the linked leaf chain making range scans one descent plus a sequential walk. Follow-ups are predictable and discriminating: how a split promotes a key (and why a leaf split copies while an internal split moves), why redistribution is preferred to merging on delete, and how concurrent descents avoid serializing on the root — latch crabbing, the safe-node rule that lets a writer release ancestors, and the latch-versus-lock distinction. The sequential-insert hot spot and the comparison against hash indexes and LSM-Trees are the usual closing questions.",
    [
      G("counts_comparisons_not_ios", "Justifying the B+Tree with 'O(log n) lookups' and treating it as a constant-factor improvement on a balanced binary tree.", "Argue in page reads: a 27-level red-black tree is up to 27 I/Os for 100M keys, while a node sized to a 16KB page gives a fanout in the hundreds and a height of 3 — with the top levels cached, a warm lookup is one read. Fanout = page size / entry size is the only lever on height.", "no mention of page size or fanout anywhere in the design; node modelled as a small object with 2-4 children; height discussed as log2(n) rather than log_m(n)."),
      G("values_in_internal_nodes", "Storing values in internal nodes (a plain B-Tree) and treating the B+ variant's leaf-only storage as an arbitrary convention.", "Values in internal nodes consume bytes that would otherwise be child pointers, lowering fanout and raising height for every lookup; leaf-only storage maximizes fanout AND lets the leaves be chained so a range scan is one descent plus a sequential walk instead of repeated root-to-leaf traversals.", "no next pointer on the leaf node; rangeScan implemented as repeated get() calls or an in-order traversal through internal nodes; internal node class carrying a values array."),
      G("split_merge_handwaved", "Describing rebalancing as 'split when full' without the promotion rule, and handling delete by ignoring underflow or merging immediately.", "State the invariant (every non-root node at least half full, all leaves at equal depth) and the asymmetry: a LEAF split copies the median up, an INTERNAL split moves it up; on delete, try redistribution from a sibling first and merge only when neither can spare a key — merges are structural, must be logged, and can cascade to the root.", "the same promotion logic used for leaf and internal splits, causing silently missing keys; no isUnderflow check; deletion that leaves nodes arbitrarily empty with no comment about vacuum or rebuild; rotations proposed as the rebalancing mechanism."),
    ],
    0.6, DIAG.bplus, "B+Tree index class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── LSM-TREE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m19", "lld_m19_t2", 2, "Design an LSM-Tree", "design-lsm-tree",
    ["case-study", "storage-engine", "write-optimized", "compaction"],
    "A metrics pipeline ingests 400,000 writes per second, each landing at a random key. A B+Tree turns that into 400,000 random page writes per second and the disk dies of seeks. An LSM-Tree absorbs the same load happily — but now a single get() may have to consult a dozen files, and a background process is permanently rewriting data you already wrote. Design the storage engine, and be precise about what you traded away to get the write throughput.",
    "The whole structure is one inversion: never update data in place; only ever APPEND. Writes go to a write-ahead log for durability and into an in-memory sorted MEMTABLE for ordering; when the memtable fills, it is flushed WHOLE as an immutable, sorted SSTABLE — one large sequential write instead of thousands of scattered ones. The price is paid on the read side and it is paid honestly: a key may live in any of many files, so a read must consult them newest-first, which is READ AMPLIFICATION, held in check by a per-table Bloom filter (a cheap 'definitely not here') and a block index. Background COMPACTION merges the runs to bound their number, and the choice between size-tiered and leveled compaction is a direct, quantifiable trade of write amplification against read and space amplification.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: an ordered key-value store with put(key, value), get(key) returning the most recent value, delete(key), and rangeScan(lo, hi) in key order — the same contract as a B+Tree index, deliberately, because the point of the topic is that the same contract admits a radically different implementation. Values are opaque bytes; keys are comparable. Durability: an acknowledged write must survive a process crash, which forces a write-ahead log because the in-memory structure is by definition volatile. Reads must reflect the newest write for a key, including a delete, which is the requirement that produces tombstones. Non-functional constraints define the design. The workload is WRITE-HEAVY with random key distribution — ingestion, event logs, time series, message metadata — so the primary objective is to convert random logical writes into SEQUENTIAL physical writes, because sequential throughput exceeds random throughput by one to two orders of magnitude on a spinning disk and still by a wide margin on an SSD, where random small writes additionally trigger flash erase-block churn and wear. Space matters but bounded overhead is acceptable. Read latency matters but is explicitly secondary — the design will accept a worse read path in exchange for the write path, and being able to state that trade as a deliberate choice rather than a defect is the point of the whole exercise. Two operational requirements shape the object model: the engine must be able to serve reads while a flush or a compaction is in progress, so files must be IMMUTABLE once written and the set of live files must be swapped atomically; and background work must be throttleable, because unbounded compaction will starve foreground traffic. Out of scope but named: replication, transactions beyond single-key atomicity, and the block cache's eviction policy."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "LSMSTORE is the facade owning the current memtable, the list of immutable memtables awaiting flush, the VERSION (the current set of SSTables organized by level or tier), and the compactor. MEMTABLE is the only mutable structure: a sorted in-memory map — a skip list in practice, because it supports concurrent inserts without a global lock and gives ordered iteration for the flush — with a size budget and an immutable flag. When it fills, it is atomically swapped for a fresh one and handed to the flusher; it is not modified again, which is what lets reads keep serving from it while it is being written out. WRITEAHEADLOG is an append-only file: every put and delete is appended (and fsynced according to the durability setting) BEFORE the memtable is updated, so a crash is recovered by replaying the log into a fresh memtable; a WAL segment is deleted only after the memtable it covers has been durably flushed. SSTABLE is the immutable on-disk sorted run and the design's centre of gravity: a sequence of sorted data BLOCKS, a sparse BLOCK INDEX mapping the first key of each block to its offset (so a lookup is one index binary search plus one block read rather than a scan), a BLOOM FILTER over the table's keys, and a footer with metadata including the min and max key. Immutability is what makes it safe to read without latching, safe to cache aggressively, and safe to delete only when no reader holds it. BLOOMFILTER answers 'is this key definitely absent' with no false negatives and a tunable false-positive rate; it is the single most important read optimization in the design. TOMBSTONE is a delete marker — a key with a special deleted flag and a sequence number — written exactly like a value, because in an append-only world a delete IS a write. COMPACTOR runs in the background, merges sorted runs, drops shadowed versions and expired tombstones, and installs a new Version atomically. VERSION is the immutable snapshot of the live file set, which is what gives readers a stable view while compaction swaps files underneath."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The write path is deliberately trivial and that is the achievement: append to the WAL, insert into the memtable, return. No tree descent, no page read, no random write — the ordering work is done in memory and the disk sees only sequential appends. The flush path converts the sorted memtable into an SSTable in ONE sequential pass, building the block index and the Bloom filter as it goes, then atomically installs a new Version and retires the WAL segment. The read path is where the design has to be careful, and the ordering rule is the invariant: consult sources NEWEST FIRST — the active memtable, then any immutable memtables not yet flushed, then SSTables from newest to oldest — and STOP at the first hit, because the first hit is by construction the most recent version of that key. A tombstone found first means the key is deleted; returning null there rather than continuing to search older tables is the correctness rule that makes deletion work at all. Before touching a table's blocks, ask its Bloom filter, and if the filter says absent, skip the file entirely without any I/O. Patterns and modelling decisions worth defending: IMMUTABILITY as the concurrency strategy — SSTables and Versions are never mutated, so readers need no locks and compaction never blocks a read; a MergingIterator (a k-way merge over per-source iterators using a small heap, with newest-wins deduplication) implements rangeScan and is reused verbatim by compaction, which is a genuinely elegant reuse and worth pointing out; a SEQUENCE NUMBER on every entry gives a total order across sources, which is what makes 'newest wins' well-defined when the same key appears in two files, and is also the foundation for snapshot reads. What is deliberately absent: in-place update anywhere, any lock held across I/O, and any attempt to keep a global sorted structure on disk — the design accepts MANY sorted runs and pays for that choice on the read path and in compaction, which is exactly the bargain the interviewer wants to hear stated."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class LsmStore {\n  private volatile MemTable active = new MemTable();\n  private final List<MemTable> flushing = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n  private volatile Version version;              // immutable snapshot of live SSTables\n  private final WriteAheadLog wal;\n  private final long memtableBudgetBytes;\n\n  void put(byte[] key, byte[] value) {\n    long seq = nextSeq();\n    wal.append(seq, key, value, false);          // durability FIRST\n    active.put(key, new Entry(seq, value, false));\n    if (active.sizeBytes() > memtableBudgetBytes) rotate();\n  }\n\n  void delete(byte[] key) {                      // a delete is a WRITE: a tombstone\n    long seq = nextSeq();\n    wal.append(seq, key, null, true);\n    active.put(key, new Entry(seq, null, true));\n  }\n\n  private synchronized void rotate() {\n    MemTable old = active;\n    old.markImmutable();\n    flushing.add(old);\n    active = new MemTable();                     // reads keep serving from `old`\n    wal.roll();\n    flusher.submit(() -> flush(old));            // sorted memtable -> ONE sequential file\n  }\n\n  byte[] get(byte[] key) {\n    Entry e = active.get(key);                              // 1. newest source first\n    if (e != null) return e.tombstone ? null : e.value;\n    for (int i = flushing.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {        // 2. not-yet-flushed memtables\n      e = flushing.get(i).get(key);\n      if (e != null) return e.tombstone ? null : e.value;\n    }\n    for (SSTable t : version.tablesNewestFirst()) {         // 3. on-disk runs, newest first\n      if (!t.mayContain(key)) continue;                     // Bloom: skip the file, ZERO I/O\n      if (key < t.minKey() || key > t.maxKey()) continue;   // (conceptual: key-range prune)\n      e = t.lookup(key);                                    // block index -> ONE block read\n      if (e != null) return e.tombstone ? null : e.value;   // STOP at first hit\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class SSTable {                            // IMMUTABLE once written\n  private final BlockIndex index;                // first key of each block -> offset (sparse)\n  private final BloomFilter bloom;               // no false negatives\n  private final byte[] minKey, maxKey;\n  boolean mayContain(byte[] k) { return bloom.mightContain(k); }\n  Entry lookup(byte[] k) {\n    int off = index.blockOffsetFor(k);           // binary search in memory\n    return Block.read(off).find(k);              // exactly one block read\n  }\n}\n\n// Compaction and rangeScan share ONE mechanism: k-way merge, newest sequence wins.\nfinal class MergingIterator implements Iterator<Entry> {\n  private final PriorityQueue<Cursor> heap;      // ordered by (key asc, seq desc)\n  // dropping a shadowed version, or a tombstone below the oldest live snapshot,\n  // is a decision made HERE — which is why compaction is where space is reclaimed.\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the write-optimized inversion — memtable + WAL versus in-place update",
        "Put the two engines side by side on the same operation and the whole topic falls out. A B+Tree PUT descends to the leaf that owns the key and modifies that page in place: the page must be read (if not cached), modified, logged, and eventually written back — and because keys arrive in random order, those pages are scattered, so the physical write pattern is RANDOM. On a spinning disk that is a seek per write, capping throughput at the low hundreds per second per disk; on an SSD, random small writes are absorbed better but still force read-modify-write of flash pages and drive write amplification inside the device itself. The LSM-Tree refuses to do that work at write time. A put appends a record to the WAL — a purely sequential file write — and inserts into the in-memory memtable, which is where the sorting happens; the operation acknowledges without touching the main data files at all. Note what has been achieved: the ORDERING work still gets done, but in RAM, where it is cheap, and the disk only ever sees appends. When the memtable exceeds its budget, it is sealed and written out in a single sequential pass as an SSTable, amortizing thousands of logical writes into one large contiguous file write. The durability story must be exact, because interviewers push here. The memtable is volatile, so the WAL is what makes the acknowledgement honest: append and (depending on the durability setting) fsync the log BEFORE applying to the memtable, and recovery replays the log tail into a fresh memtable. The tunable is where the sync happens — fsync per write is the strongest and slowest, group commit (batch several writes into one fsync) is the standard compromise, and 'no sync, rely on the OS' trades a small window of acknowledged-but-lost writes for throughput; naming the three and their failure semantics is the senior answer. A WAL segment can be deleted only once every memtable it covers has been durably flushed, which is why the WAL is rolled at the same moment the memtable is rotated. Two consequences of the inversion are worth stating explicitly. First, IMMUTABILITY is not incidental: because an SSTable is never modified after it is written, readers need no synchronization against writers, files can be cached and memory-mapped freely, and background compaction can produce new files without ever blocking foreground traffic — the concurrency story is dramatically simpler than the B+Tree's latch crabbing, and that simplicity is part of what you bought. Second, the work did not disappear — it MOVED. The B+Tree pays its cost at write time and keeps the read path short; the LSM-Tree defers the cost to a background merge and to every subsequent read that must consider multiple runs. There is no free lunch, only a choice about when to pay, and the workload decides which schedule is cheaper."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: read amplification — the cost of the bargain, and the two structures that pay it down",
        "Because a key may have been written at any time, it may live in the active memtable, in a memtable awaiting flush, or in any SSTable on disk, and the current value is whichever copy is newest. A naive read therefore consults every source until it finds a hit, and if the key does not exist it consults ALL of them — that is READ AMPLIFICATION, the number of sources examined per logical read, and it is the honest cost of the write path. Worst case, with T sorted runs, a lookup for an absent key touches T files, and a negative lookup is the common case in many workloads (checking whether a row exists before inserting, or a cache miss path), so it cannot be dismissed. Two structures pay it down and both belong in the design. The BLOOM FILTER is the big one: a per-SSTable bit array with k hash functions, built at flush time, answering 'might this key be present' with NO FALSE NEGATIVES and a tunable false-positive rate — roughly 1% at 10 bits per key, roughly 0.1% at 15. If the filter says absent, the file is skipped with ZERO disk I/O. The arithmetic is what makes the design viable: with ten runs and a 1% false-positive rate, an absent key triggers on average about 0.1 wasted block reads instead of ten, so read amplification for negative lookups collapses to near one. The properties to state precisely are that false positives cost only a wasted block read (never a wrong answer) and that false negatives would be a correctness bug, which is why the direction of the guarantee matters and why the filter must be built from exactly the keys in the file. Say the memory cost out loud too — around 10 bits per key means roughly 1.2GB of filters per billion keys, which is why filters for the largest, coldest level are sometimes dropped or given fewer bits. The second structure is the BLOCK INDEX: an SSTable is a sequence of blocks, and a sparse index in memory maps the first key of each block to its file offset, so a lookup inside a table that passes the Bloom filter is one in-memory binary search plus exactly ONE block read — not a scan of the file. Sparse rather than dense is deliberate: one entry per block instead of per key keeps the index small enough to stay resident. A third, cheaper filter is the min/max KEY RANGE in the footer, which prunes whole files for range-disjoint queries for free. Now the honest limits. Bloom filters do NOT help range scans — a scan must merge across all runs whose key range overlaps the query, so scan cost grows with the number of runs, which is precisely why leveled compaction (which guarantees non-overlapping runs within a level) is preferred for scan-heavy workloads. And a get() must STOP at the first hit, tombstone included: finding a tombstone and continuing to older files would resurrect deleted data, the single most common LSM correctness bug and a favourite interview probe."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: compaction strategies, write amplification, and tombstones",
        "Without compaction the number of runs grows without bound, read amplification grows with it, and deleted and overwritten data is never reclaimed — so COMPACTION is not an optimization, it is what keeps the structure viable. A compaction merges several sorted runs into fewer, using the same k-way MergingIterator the range scan uses, keeping only the newest version of each key and dropping data that is no longer reachable. The two strategies differ in what they merge and produce a trade-off you should be able to quantify. SIZE-TIERED compaction groups runs of SIMILAR SIZE and merges them into one larger run: with a tier fanout T, a given piece of data is rewritten roughly log_T(N) times over its life, so WRITE AMPLIFICATION is low — this is the write-optimized end. The cost is that runs within the store may have OVERLAPPING key ranges, so several must be consulted per read (read amplification stays high, mitigated by Bloom filters for point lookups but not for scans), and SPACE AMPLIFICATION is bad: merging several large runs requires enough free space to hold the output alongside the inputs, and in the worst case a store can transiently need close to twice its logical size, while obsolete versions linger in older tiers. LEVELED compaction organizes data into levels L0, L1, L2 with each level about T times larger than the last, and — the key property — within a level (below L0) the runs have DISJOINT key ranges, so a point lookup touches at most one file per level and a range scan merges at most one run per level. Read amplification and space amplification are both excellent (obsolete data is reclaimed quickly, overhead typically around 10%). The price is WRITE AMPLIFICATION: pushing a file from L(n) into L(n+1) requires rewriting every overlapping file in the larger level, so a byte can be rewritten roughly T times per level and about T times log_T(N) overall — commonly 10 to 30x, which on an SSD is both a throughput tax and a wear tax. State the summary as a triangle: read amplification, write amplification, and space amplification cannot all be minimized at once, and a compaction strategy picks a corner. Size-tiered for ingest-heavy workloads with few reads; leveled for read-and-scan-heavy workloads that can afford background write bandwidth; hybrids (leveled above, tiered below, or tiered-with-a-leveled-top) exist precisely because most real workloads sit between. TOMBSTONES deserve their own treatment because they are the most subtle correctness surface. A delete cannot modify the older file that holds the value — files are immutable — so it writes a marker, and until every older copy of that key has been merged away the tombstone MUST be retained; drop it early and the old value resurrects. That produces three real consequences: a delete temporarily makes the store BIGGER, not smaller; a tombstone can only be discarded during a compaction that includes the OLDEST run containing that key (and no live snapshot older than the tombstone), which for leveled compaction means it must reach the bottom level; and mass deletion of a key range leaves a dense band of tombstones that a range scan must read and discard, so a scan over a deleted region can be catastrophically slow while returning nothing — the classic 'my empty queue table takes 30 seconds to scan' production incident. The mitigations to name are range tombstones (one marker for an interval instead of per key) and TTL-aware compaction that prioritizes files dense in tombstones."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "LSM-TREE vs B+TREE: the decision is workload-shaped and should be stated as such. The B+Tree updates in place, giving low and predictable read amplification (about tree height, usually one warm page read) and cheap range scans over a single structure, at the cost of random writes and a contended rightmost page under sequential keys. The LSM-Tree converts random writes into sequential ones and absorbs far higher ingest, at the cost of read amplification, background compaction consuming disk bandwidth and CPU, and latency variance when a large compaction runs. Write-heavy ingestion, time series, and event logs take the LSM; read-heavy OLTP with strict tail-latency requirements takes the B+Tree. SIZE-TIERED vs LEVELED: low write amplification and high read/space amplification versus the reverse; the numbers to quote are roughly log_T(N) rewrites for tiered against roughly T·log_T(N) for leveled, and near-2x transient space for tiered against roughly 10% overhead for leveled. MEMTABLE STRUCTURE: a skip list is the standard choice because it supports lock-free concurrent inserts and ordered iteration; a balanced tree gives the same ordering but is harder to make concurrent; a hash map is faster for point lookups but cannot produce the sorted flush without a sort pass, which defeats the purpose. DURABILITY TUNING: fsync per write, group commit, or asynchronous — three points on a curve with explicit failure semantics, and picking one is a product decision, not a technical default. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE, honestly: compaction is a background process competing with foreground traffic for the same disk, so an under-throttled compactor causes latency spikes and an over-throttled one lets runs accumulate until reads collapse — this feedback loop is the operational reality of every LSM engine and mentioning it is production scar tissue; and Bloom filter memory grows linearly with key count, so the largest level's filters are the first thing to become unaffordable. Extension points: PER-LEVEL Bloom bit budgets, BLOCK COMPRESSION (LSM data is written once and read many times, which suits compression far better than a mutable B+Tree page does), a separate value log for large values (key-value separation, as in WiscKey) so compaction moves only keys, and SNAPSHOT reads, which fall out almost free because sequence numbers plus immutable files mean a snapshot is just a sequence number plus a pin on the current Version."),
    ],
    "The LSM-Tree is the standard follow-up to the B+Tree and it is graded on whether you can name what you traded rather than on whether you can recite the components. The strong answer opens with the inversion — never update in place, append to a WAL and an in-memory sorted memtable, flush whole immutable SSTables so the disk sees only sequential writes — and then immediately volunteers the cost: a key may live in many files, so reads amplify. The follow-ups are reliably (a) how a read stays affordable, where the expected answer is per-table Bloom filters with no false negatives plus a sparse block index, and the observation that filters do nothing for range scans; (b) size-tiered versus leveled compaction with the write/read/space amplification triangle stated in numbers; and (c) how deletion works without mutating a file, which pulls in tombstones, why they must survive until the oldest copy is merged away, and why scanning a mass-deleted range is slow. Conflating a delete with an in-place removal, or claiming Bloom filters make reads as cheap as a B+Tree's, are the two answers that end the line of questioning badly.",
    [
      G("delete_as_removal", "Treating delete as removing the key, and letting a get() that finds a tombstone continue searching older SSTables.", "Files are immutable, so a delete is a WRITE — a tombstone with a sequence number. A read must stop at the first hit in newest-first order, tombstone included, or deleted data resurrects; and the tombstone may only be dropped by a compaction that includes the oldest run holding that key with no older live snapshot.", "get() that skips tombstones and keeps scanning; deletes assumed to free space immediately; no discussion of why a mass-deleted key range makes range scans slow."),
      G("bloom_solves_everything", "Claiming Bloom filters make LSM reads as cheap as B+Tree reads, with no mention of what they do not cover.", "Bloom filters collapse read amplification for POINT lookups (about 0.1 wasted block reads across ten runs at a 1% false-positive rate) but do nothing for RANGE scans, which must merge every run whose key range overlaps — which is exactly why leveled compaction, with disjoint runs per level, is chosen for scan-heavy workloads.", "range scan described as consulting one file; no block index in the design so a passing filter implies a file scan; no mention of the roughly 10 bits per key memory cost at the largest level."),
      G("compaction_as_detail", "Describing compaction as 'a background merge' without naming a strategy or quantifying what it costs.", "Name the triangle: size-tiered gives low write amplification (about log_T(N) rewrites) but overlapping runs and near-2x transient space; leveled gives disjoint runs per level (one file per level per lookup, about 10% space overhead) at roughly T·log_T(N) write amplification. The strategy picks a corner; you cannot minimize all three.", "no strategy named; write amplification never mentioned; compaction assumed free rather than competing with foreground traffic for the same disk bandwidth."),
    ],
    0.6, DIAG.lsm, "LSM-Tree storage engine class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CONSISTENT HASHING RING ──────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m19", "lld_m19_t3", 3, "Design a Consistent Hashing Ring", "design-consistent-hashing",
    ["case-study", "distributed", "partitioning", "data-structure"],
    "Ten cache servers, keys assigned by hash(key) % 10. You add an eleventh. Roughly what fraction of your keys now map to a different server — and therefore miss? The answer is about 91%, and the resulting stampede onto the origin database is a genuine, repeatedly-observed outage. Design the ring that turns that 91% into 9%, and explain why the ring alone is not enough.",
    "Modular hashing binds every key's placement to N, so changing N re-derives every assignment. Consistent hashing breaks that binding: hash the KEYS and the NODES into the same circular space, and a key belongs to the first node clockwise from it. Now adding a node only steals the arc between it and its predecessor — about K/N keys move, and no other pair of nodes is affected. But the plain ring has a fatal flaw you must volunteer: N random points on a circle produce wildly uneven arcs, and removing a node dumps its ENTIRE share on one successor. VIRTUAL NODES — each physical node hashed to hundreds of ring positions — are what make the structure actually usable, not a refinement.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: given a key, return the node responsible for it — get(key) -> Node — with a stable, deterministic answer that every client computes identically without coordination, since the whole point is that clients route without asking a metadata service on each request. The ring must support addNode(node) and removeNode(node) at runtime, because nodes are added for capacity and removed by failure or decommission, and both operations must move as few keys as possible. It must support WEIGHTED nodes, because fleets are heterogeneous — a machine with twice the memory should own roughly twice the keyspace. It must support REPLICATION: for a key, return an ordered list of R nodes (a preference list) so a value can be stored on several machines, and those R nodes must be R DISTINCT PHYSICAL machines, which is a constraint the naive implementation violates the moment virtual nodes exist. Non-functional constraints drive the design. Lookup is on the hot path of every request, so it must be fast — O(log V) with a small constant, where V is the total number of ring positions — and allocation-free. The mapping must be uniform enough that no node carries a disproportionate share: the target is that the busiest node's load stays within a small factor of the mean, and quantifying that is part of the design rather than a hope. Membership changes must MINIMIZE MOVEMENT: the metric is the fraction of keys whose owner changes, and the design goal is K/N rather than the naive (N-1)/N. The structure must be safe for concurrent reads while membership changes, which given how rarely membership changes suggests copy-on-write over locking. Explicitly out of scope: failure detection and gossip (the ring consumes a membership list, it does not produce one), and the data movement itself — the ring says WHICH keys move, not how they are transferred."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "CONSISTENTHASHRING is the facade and owns the sorted ring structure plus the lookup, add, and remove operations. The RING itself is best modelled as a sorted map from a 64-bit (or 128-bit) position to the owning virtual node — a TreeMap in Java, or a sorted long array with binary search when allocation and cache behaviour matter — because the one query the structure must answer is a CEILING search: given the key's hash, find the smallest ring position greater than or equal to it, wrapping to the first entry when the key hashes past the last node. PHYSICALNODE is the real machine: an id, an endpoint, a weight, and (usually) a rack or zone tag used later for replica placement. VIRTUALNODE is the ring citizen: a (physicalNode, replicaIndex) pair whose ring position is hash(nodeId + separator + replicaIndex). The distinction matters in the object model because the ring is keyed by virtual node positions but every answer must be resolved back to a PHYSICAL node — the map's value should therefore be the physical node reference, with the replica index only participating in position derivation. HASHFUNCTION is a genuine collaborator, not an incidental choice: it must distribute uniformly over the whole space (MD5 truncated, MurmurHash3, or xxHash are the standard picks) and it must be STABLE across processes and versions, because two clients that disagree about a node's ring position disagree about ownership, which is a silent data-placement bug. Explicitly do not use the language's default object hash — Java's String.hashCode is neither uniform enough nor guaranteed stable across implementations for this purpose. PREFERENCELIST is the ordered result of a successor walk: R distinct physical nodes starting at the key's owner. A RINGSNAPSHOT (immutable, swapped atomically on membership change) is the concurrency strategy: readers never lock, they read a volatile reference to an immutable structure."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design is small and every part of it is justified by one of the two failure modes it exists to prevent. The core is a sorted structure of ring positions plus a ceiling query with wraparound — three lines of logic, and the reason the topic is worth an interview is everything around those three lines. FIRST DECISION: the value stored at each ring position is the PHYSICAL node, and the number of positions per physical node is proportional to its WEIGHT. That single decision gives weighted placement for free — a node with weight 2 simply gets twice as many virtual positions and therefore owns roughly twice the arc — and it means the add/remove path is 'insert or delete this node's V positions', with no rebalancing of anything else. SECOND DECISION: use an immutable snapshot. Membership changes are rare (minutes to days apart) and lookups are constant, so copy-on-write is exactly right: build a new sorted map with the node's positions added or removed and publish it through a volatile field. Readers get a consistent view with zero synchronization, and there is no window where a lookup sees a half-updated ring — which, if it existed, would route a key to a node that does not own it. A lock or a concurrent map would pay a cost on every lookup to optimize an operation that happens once a day. THIRD DECISION: replication is a successor WALK, not R independent hashes. Starting from the key's owning position, step clockwise collecting virtual nodes and keeping only those whose PHYSICAL node is not already in the list, until R distinct physicals are collected. This preserves the movement property (adding a node perturbs only the preference lists of nearby keys) and is the only formulation that is correct in the presence of virtual nodes. Complexity: lookup is O(log V) for the ceiling search; the preference walk is O(R) expected steps but its worst case depends on the virtual node layout, which is worth flagging honestly. Deliberately absent: any coordination on the lookup path, any per-key state, and any attempt to make the ring itself detect failure — membership is an input."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class ConsistentHashRing {\n  private final HashFunction hash;              // Murmur3 / MD5 — uniform AND stable across processes\n  private final int vnodesPerUnitWeight;       // 100-500 in practice; see the load-variance argument\n  private volatile NavigableMap<Long, PhysicalNode> ring = new TreeMap<>();   // immutable snapshot\n\n  PhysicalNode get(String key) {\n    NavigableMap<Long, PhysicalNode> r = ring;               // read the snapshot ONCE\n    if (r.isEmpty()) return null;\n    long h = hash.of(key);\n    Map.Entry<Long, PhysicalNode> e = r.ceilingEntry(h);     // first node clockwise\n    return (e != null ? e : r.firstEntry()).getValue();      // WRAPAROUND past the last position\n  }\n\n  // Preference list: walk clockwise collecting R DISTINCT PHYSICAL nodes.\n  List<PhysicalNode> preferenceList(String key, int r) {\n    NavigableMap<Long, PhysicalNode> snap = ring;\n    List<PhysicalNode> out = new ArrayList<>(r);\n    if (snap.isEmpty()) return out;\n    long h = hash.of(key);\n    // tailMap from the key's position, then wrap through the whole ring once\n    for (int pass = 0; pass < 2 && out.size() < r; pass++) {\n      for (PhysicalNode n : (pass == 0 ? snap.tailMap(h, true) : snap.headMap(h, false)).values()) {\n        if (!out.contains(n)) out.add(n);                    // distinct PHYSICAL, not virtual\n        if (out.size() == r) break;\n      }\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n\n  synchronized void addNode(PhysicalNode n) {                // copy-on-write: rare, so pay here\n    TreeMap<Long, PhysicalNode> next = new TreeMap<>(ring);\n    for (int i = 0; i < vnodes(n); i++) next.put(hash.of(n.id() + \"#\" + i), n);\n    ring = next;                                             // atomic publish; readers never lock\n  }\n\n  synchronized void removeNode(PhysicalNode n) {\n    TreeMap<Long, PhysicalNode> next = new TreeMap<>(ring);\n    for (int i = 0; i < vnodes(n); i++) next.remove(hash.of(n.id() + \"#\" + i));\n    ring = next;\n  }\n\n  private int vnodes(PhysicalNode n) { return (int) (n.weight() * vnodesPerUnitWeight); }\n}\n\n// The contrast the whole design exists to defeat:\n//   naive:  node = nodes[ hash(key) % nodes.size() ]   // N changes -> ~(N-1)/N of keys move\n//   ring:   only the arc between the new node and its predecessor changes owner (~K/N keys)"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the numbers — why hash % N is a redistribution catastrophe",
        "State the problem quantitatively, because 'consistent hashing moves fewer keys' is a slogan and the interviewer wants the arithmetic. With node = hash(key) % N, a key's owner is a function of N itself, so changing N re-derives EVERY assignment. Go from 10 nodes to 11: a key stays put only if hash(key) % 10 equals hash(key) % 11, which for uniformly distributed hashes happens for roughly 1 in 11 keys — about 9% stay, about 91% move. Generalize: moving from N to N+1 relocates approximately N/(N+1) of the keyspace, so the larger your fleet, the WORSE the disruption of adding one machine, which is precisely backwards from what you want. Now play out the operational consequence, because that is what makes this a real incident rather than a curiosity: if this is a cache tier, 91% of keys suddenly miss, every miss falls through to the origin database, and the database receives a near-simultaneous multiple of its normal read load — a thundering herd that has taken down real systems, and the reason a routine capacity addition becomes an outage. If it is a sharded data store, 91% of the DATA must physically move, which is hours of network transfer during which the cluster is degraded. Consistent hashing changes the binding. Map both keys and nodes into the same circular space of size 2^64 with the same hash function, and define ownership as 'the first node encountered walking clockwise from the key's position'. A key's owner is now determined by the node POSITIONS, not by the count, so adding a node inserts one point on the circle and captures exactly the arc between its predecessor and itself — every key outside that arc keeps its owner, untouched. In expectation that arc is 1/N of the circle, so about K/N keys move where K is the total number of keys — with 10 nodes, about 9% instead of 91%, a factor of ten, and the improvement grows with fleet size instead of shrinking. Removal is the mirror: the departing node's arc is absorbed by its clockwise successor and nothing else changes. Two properties are worth naming precisely because they are what the word 'consistent' refers to: MONOTONICITY — when a node is added, keys only ever move from an old node to the NEW node, never between two old nodes, so the movement is minimal and the transfer plan is trivially derived; and LOCALITY of change — the only nodes whose responsibilities change are the immediate neighbours of the change, so a membership update is not a global event. It is also worth stating what consistent hashing does NOT solve: it says nothing about whether the load is EVEN, only about how much of it moves when membership changes. That is the next deep dive, and volunteering the distinction is exactly the signal that separates someone who has read the paper from someone who has deployed the thing."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: virtual nodes are mandatory, not an optimization",
        "Take the plain ring literally — one ring position per physical node — and it fails in two ways that make it unusable in production, which is why every real implementation uses virtual nodes and why presenting them as a nice-to-have is the tell of a shallow answer. FIRST FAILURE, LOAD VARIANCE. Placing N points uniformly at random on a circle does not divide it into N equal arcs; it produces arcs whose lengths follow roughly an exponential distribution, so the spread is severe. Concretely, with N random points the LARGEST arc is on the order of ln(N)/N of the circle rather than 1/N — so with 10 nodes the unluckiest node can easily own two to three times the mean share, and the whole fleet must then be provisioned for that node's load, wasting capacity everywhere else. This is not a tail risk you can ignore; it is the expected outcome of a single random draw, and it is fixed at ring-construction time, so a badly-drawn ring is badly-drawn until membership changes. SECOND FAILURE, and the more dangerous one, THE REMOVAL AVALANCHE. With one position per node, a node's entire arc has exactly ONE clockwise successor, so when that node fails, 100% of its keys land on a single machine. That machine now serves roughly double its designed load — and if it was already near capacity, it fails too, handing its own now-doubled share to the NEXT successor: a cascading failure that walks the ring and takes down the tier. The desired behaviour is the opposite — a failed node's load should spread across ALL surviving nodes, so each absorbs only 1/(N-1) extra. VIRTUAL NODES deliver both. Hash each physical node to V positions instead of one (hash(nodeId + '#' + i) for i in 0..V), and store the physical node at each. Now each physical node owns V small scattered arcs instead of one large contiguous one. The load-variance effect is a straightforward averaging argument: the coefficient of variation of a node's total share falls roughly as 1/sqrt(V), so with V around 100 to 200 the spread across nodes drops to a few percent — the standard practical range, and the number to quote. The avalanche disappears for a structural reason worth stating separately: the V arcs of a failed node have V DIFFERENT clockwise successors scattered around the ring, so its load is divided among many survivors rather than dumped on one. Virtual nodes buy two more things almost incidentally. WEIGHTING becomes free — give a node twice the virtual positions and it owns roughly twice the keyspace, so a heterogeneous fleet needs no special mechanism. And ADDING a node draws its new keys from many existing nodes in small slices rather than carving one large chunk out of a single neighbour, which means the data-transfer load during a scale-out is spread across the fleet instead of hammering one machine. The costs are honest and small: memory is O(N·V) ring entries (with 200 nodes and 200 vnodes that is 40,000 entries — trivial), lookup becomes O(log(N·V)) which for these sizes is about 16 comparisons, and the preference-list walk must now explicitly skip virtual nodes belonging to a physical node already in the list, which is the correctness detail most naive implementations get wrong and which silently places all R replicas of a key on ONE machine."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: lookup as a ceiling search, replication walks, and the hash function contract",
        "The lookup deserves care because it is the only operation on the hot path. The question 'which node owns hash h' is 'find the smallest ring position at least h', which is a CEILING query, and the structure must support it in logarithmic time: a TreeMap gives ceilingEntry directly, and a sorted long array with Arrays.binarySearch gives the same asymptotics with far better cache behaviour and no per-lookup allocation — worth choosing when the ring is queried millions of times per second and rebuilt daily. The WRAPAROUND case is the one-line bug every first implementation ships: if the key hashes beyond the last node's position there is no ceiling, and the correct answer is the FIRST entry, because the space is a circle. Returning null or throwing there means roughly 1/V of all keys fail, which is a low enough rate to escape a quick test and high enough to page someone. REPLICATION is the second subtlety. The preference list for a key is the R nodes that should hold it, obtained by continuing the clockwise walk from the owner. With virtual nodes, consecutive ring positions frequently belong to the SAME physical node, so a naive 'take the next R entries' returns a list with duplicates — meaning the R copies of a key live on fewer than R machines, and in the worst case all on one, which silently destroys the durability guarantee the replication existed to provide. The walk must therefore collect DISTINCT PHYSICAL nodes, skipping repeats, as in the code. Production rings go one step further with a rack- or zone-aware filter: skip a candidate whose failure domain is already represented, so the R replicas do not share a rack or an availability zone — the same walk with a stronger distinctness predicate, which is a clean extension precisely because the walk was written as a filtered traversal rather than an index arithmetic trick. Note also that this walk is what makes failover cheap: when the owner is down, the client simply uses the next entry in the already-computed preference list, and when the node returns, ownership reverts because the ring is deterministic. THE HASH FUNCTION is a contract, not a detail. It must be UNIFORM over the output space, or the ring's arcs are skewed no matter how many virtual nodes you add — clustering in the hash output translates directly into clustering on the circle. It must be STABLE: the same string must produce the same 64-bit value in every process, every language binding, and every version, forever, because the ring is computed independently by many clients and any disagreement means two clients route the same key to different nodes — a silent split-brain in data placement that produces missing reads rather than errors. This rules out Java's String.hashCode (32 bits, poor distribution, and not guaranteed stable across implementations) and any hash seeded per-process. MD5 truncated to 64 bits is the classic choice (cryptographic strength is unnecessary but the distribution is excellent), MurmurHash3 and xxHash are the modern ones for speed. Finally, a comparison worth having ready: RENDEZVOUS HASHING (highest random weight) solves the same problem differently — hash the pair (key, node) for every node and pick the maximum — giving perfectly even distribution and minimal movement with NO virtual nodes and no ring structure at all, at the cost of an O(N) computation per lookup rather than O(log(N·V)). For small N it is simpler and better; for large N or when you need an ordered preference list cheaply, the ring wins. JUMP CONSISTENT HASH is a third point: O(log N) time, no memory, perfectly balanced, but it can only map to bucket indices 0..N-1 and cannot express arbitrary node removal, so it fits shard-count changes rather than a mutable fleet."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "RING vs RENDEZVOUS HASHING: rendezvous computes hash(key, node) for all N nodes and takes the max, which gives near-perfect balance and minimal movement with no virtual nodes, no ring to build, and a trivially derived ordered preference list (sort by weight) — but it costs O(N) hashes per lookup, so it wins for small or moderate N and loses for large fleets or extreme lookup rates, where the ring's O(log(N·V)) with a cache-friendly sorted array is better. RING vs JUMP CONSISTENT HASH: jump hash is O(log N) time with zero memory and perfect balance, but its buckets are indices 0..N-1, so it handles growing or shrinking the COUNT and cannot express 'remove node 7 specifically' — right for fixed shard sets, wrong for a fleet with failures. VIRTUAL NODE COUNT: more virtual nodes means smoother load (variance falling roughly as 1/sqrt(V)) and finer-grained data movement, at the cost of memory, a slightly deeper search, and a longer preference walk; 100 to 500 per node is the practical band, and pushing V very high has diminishing returns while making the ring rebuild noticeably slower. TREEMAP vs SORTED ARRAY: the TreeMap is easier to mutate incrementally; the sorted array with binary search is faster and allocation-free per lookup and pairs naturally with the copy-on-write snapshot strategy, which is the right default when membership changes daily and lookups happen constantly. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: the ring assumes keys are uniformly distributed, and a HOT KEY defeats it completely — no partitioning scheme helps when a single key carries a disproportionate share, and the fixes are outside the ring (key splitting with a suffix, a per-node local cache in front, or dedicated handling for known hot keys); saying this clearly is important because candidates often try to solve hot keys with more virtual nodes, which does nothing. Also, every client must agree on the membership list AND the hash function version, so ring configuration becomes a distributed-configuration problem, and a rolling deploy that changes either one splits the cluster's view of ownership. Extension points worth naming: ZONE-AWARE preference lists (the same walk with a failure-domain predicate), BOUNDED-LOAD consistent hashing, which caps any node at a small factor above the mean by overflowing to the next node when a node is at capacity — the standard answer when even virtual-node smoothing is not tight enough — and incremental data MIGRATION driven directly by the ring diff, which is easy precisely because the monotonicity property guarantees keys only move to the new node."),
    ],
    "Consistent hashing is asked in almost every distributed-systems-flavoured design interview, and it is graded in two stages. The first stage is whether you can state the problem NUMERICALLY: hash % N remaps roughly N/(N+1) of the keyspace when a node is added — about 91% at ten nodes — and the operational consequence is a cache-miss stampede onto the origin, while the ring moves only about K/N. Candidates who answer 'it distributes keys evenly' have missed the point entirely; even distribution is not what the structure is for. The second stage, and the real discriminator, is whether VIRTUAL NODES are presented as mandatory with the two reasons stated — severe load variance from N random points on a circle, and the removal avalanche where one successor inherits a failed node's entire share and cascades — rather than as a refinement. Expect follow-ups on the wraparound case, on replication as a successor walk collecting R DISTINCT PHYSICAL nodes (the bug that silently puts every replica on one machine), on the hash function's stability contract across clients, and on the comparison with rendezvous and jump consistent hashing.",
    [
      G("even_distribution_framing", "Explaining consistent hashing as a way to distribute keys evenly, with no numbers for what it actually optimizes.", "State the metric: hash % N relocates about N/(N+1) of keys when N changes (roughly 91% at ten nodes, with a cache stampede onto the origin), while the ring relocates about K/N because ownership depends on node POSITIONS, not the node count — and note that evenness is a separate problem the ring alone does not solve.", "no percentages anywhere in the answer; 'consistent hashing balances load' offered as the definition; no mention of what happens to a cache tier at the moment of the change."),
      G("virtual_nodes_optional", "Presenting virtual nodes as an optional optimization, or omitting them, leaving one ring position per physical node.", "They are mandatory for two structural reasons: N random points give arcs whose largest is on the order of ln(N)/N, so one node owns several times the mean; and a failed node's single arc has exactly ONE successor, which inherits 100% of its load and can cascade. With 100-500 vnodes, variance falls roughly as 1/sqrt(V) and a failure spreads across many survivors — and weighting becomes free.", "one ring entry per node; no discussion of what happens to load when a node is removed; weighting proposed as a separate special mechanism instead of a vnode count."),
      G("replica_walk_not_distinct", "Building the replica list by taking the next R ring entries, without checking that they map to distinct physical nodes.", "With virtual nodes, consecutive positions often belong to the SAME machine, so the naive walk can place every replica of a key on one host — silently voiding the durability the replication existed for. Walk clockwise collecting R DISTINCT PHYSICAL nodes, and extend the same predicate to skip failure domains already represented.", "preferenceList implemented as index arithmetic over ring entries; no distinctness check; replication described without mentioning virtual node collisions; also missing wraparound to firstEntry() when the key hashes past the last position."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.ring, "Consistent hashing ring class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── QUADTREE (SPATIAL INDEX) ─────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m19", "lld_m19_t4", 4, "Design a Quadtree (Spatial Index)", "design-quadtree",
    ["case-study", "spatial-index", "tree", "geo"],
    "Two million driver locations, and every rider request asks 'who is within 2km of me?' A B+Tree on latitude finds everyone in a band across the entire planet; a B+Tree on longitude does the same vertically; intersecting the two still scans far too much, because ordinary indexes are one-dimensional and proximity is not. Design the structure that makes a 2km box cost a few dozen node visits — and be honest about the data distribution that destroys it.",
    "A quadtree indexes SPACE, not values: each node owns an axis-aligned rectangle and, when it holds more than a threshold of items, splits into four equal quadrants and pushes the items down. The entire speedup comes from one operation — PRUNING: if a child's rectangle does not intersect the query range, that whole subtree is skipped without inspecting a single item. Two knobs govern everything, the split threshold and the max depth, and the second exists because real-world spatial data is savagely clustered — every driver in a city, none in the ocean — and a structure that subdivides until buckets are small will subdivide forever on a thousand points at the same coordinate.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: index a set of items positioned in 2D and support insert(item), remove(item), and the two queries that justify the structure — RANGE QUERY (return every item inside an axis-aligned rectangle, or a circle implemented as a bounding-box query plus an exact distance filter) and NEAREST NEIGHBOUR / k-NEAREST (return the closest items to a point). Items may be POINTS (a driver's location, a map pin) or REGIONS with their own bounding box (a delivery zone, a rendered sprite), and the design must state which it supports because the two lead to materially different node contracts. Updates must be supported, not just bulk loading: in the ride-hailing case every driver reports a new position every few seconds, so the structure must handle a high rate of MOVE operations, which is a remove plus an insert unless something smarter is done. Non-functional constraints shape the design. The query must be sub-linear in practice for typical queries — a 2km box out of a metropolitan area should visit a number of nodes proportional to the answer size plus the tree depth, not scan the dataset. Memory must be bounded and predictable: a structure whose node count can explode on adversarial input is not deployable. The real data distribution is CLUSTERED and non-uniform by nature — dense downtowns, empty countryside, and many items at literally identical coordinates (a fleet parked in one depot) — and the design must survive that rather than assume uniformity. Concurrency: reads dominate heavily, so the design should say how it serves queries while positions update, which usually points at either a coarse read-write lock per subtree or a periodically rebuilt immutable snapshot. Out of scope: geodesic distance corrections, projection choice, and persistence — but the design should note that the true-distance filter belongs at the end of the query, not inside the traversal."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "BOUNDARY is the value object doing the real work and it deserves to be named first: an axis-aligned bounding box (x, y, halfWidth, halfHeight, or minX/minY/maxX/maxY) with exactly three methods — contains(point), intersects(otherBox), and a quadrant subdivision producing four child boxes. Every pruning decision in the structure is one intersects() call, so this class is the hot path and should be primitive-field, allocation-free, and free of any geo semantics. QUADNODE owns a Boundary, a DEPTH, a bucket of ITEMS, and either null children (a leaf) or an array of exactly four children in a fixed NW/NE/SW/SE order. Its invariants are the specification: a leaf holds at most splitThreshold items UNLESS it is at maxDepth, in which case its bucket may grow without limit; and a node's items are always geometrically inside its boundary. QUADTREE is the facade holding the root, the two tuning parameters, and the query entry points; it also owns the item-to-node index (a HashMap from item id to its current node) that makes remove and move O(depth) instead of a search. SPATIALITEM is the stored payload: an id, a position, and — if regions are supported — its own bounding box; keeping the item's geometry on the item rather than assuming a bare point is what lets the same tree index shapes. For nearest-neighbour the design adds a small transient collaborator: a bounded MAX-HEAP of the k best candidates found so far, whose current worst distance is the radius used to prune, plus a MIN-HEAP of nodes ordered by their box's minimum possible distance to the query point, which is what turns nearest-neighbour from 'expanding range queries' into a single best-first traversal. Naming both heaps and what each is ordered by is the difference between describing the algorithm and having implemented it."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The structure is a recursive spatial decomposition and the class design should make the recursion obvious: a node either holds items or delegates to four children, so insert, remove, and query are each a short recursive method with the same shape — test the boundary, then either act locally or recurse into the relevant children. INSERT: if the point is not within this node's boundary, return false immediately (this is also the top-level bounds check); if the node is a leaf with room, or is at maxDepth, add to the bucket; otherwise subdivide if not already subdivided and hand the item to the child that contains it. The SUBDIVIDE step must redistribute the existing bucket into the new children, and forgetting that is a classic bug that leaves items stranded in an internal node where queries may or may not look for them. QUERY: if this node's boundary does not intersect the range, return — the prune, and the entire reason the structure is fast; otherwise test the local bucket items against the range, then recurse into every child (each of which prunes itself). Note the asymmetry with a B+Tree: a spatial query may descend into MULTIPLE children, because a rectangle can straddle quadrant lines, so there is no single path and the complexity analysis is different in kind. NEAREST NEIGHBOUR is best-first, not a range query in disguise: push the root onto a min-heap ordered by the box's minimum distance to the query point, repeatedly pop the most promising node, and stop as soon as the popped node's minimum possible distance exceeds the current k-th best actual distance — a correct and tight termination condition. Tuning parameters are a design decision to be defended, not constants to be guessed: splitThreshold is typically 4 to 16 (too small and the tree is deep with pointer-chasing overhead per item; too large and leaves degrade into linear scans), and maxDepth exists solely to bound the damage from clustering. Deliberately absent: any attempt to keep the tree balanced by rotation — a quadtree's shape is determined entirely by the data's spatial distribution, and that is both its strength and the source of its worst case."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "record Boundary(double cx, double cy, double hw, double hh) {          // axis-aligned box\n  boolean contains(double x, double y) {\n    return x >= cx - hw && x < cx + hw && y >= cy - hh && y < cy + hh;   // half-open: no double-counting\n  }\n  boolean intersects(Boundary o) {                                       // the PRUNING test\n    return Math.abs(cx - o.cx) <= hw + o.hw && Math.abs(cy - o.cy) <= hh + o.hh;\n  }\n  Boundary quadrant(int i) {                                             // 0=NW 1=NE 2=SW 3=SE\n    double qw = hw / 2, qh = hh / 2;\n    double x = cx + ((i == 1 || i == 3) ? qw : -qw);\n    double y = cy + ((i >= 2) ? -qh : qh);\n    return new Boundary(x, y, qw, qh);\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class QuadNode {\n  final Boundary boundary; final int depth;\n  final List<SpatialItem> items = new ArrayList<>();\n  QuadNode[] children;                    // null = leaf\n  QuadNode(Boundary b, int d) { boundary = b; depth = d; }\n  boolean isLeaf() { return children == null; }\n}\n\nfinal class Quadtree {\n  private final QuadNode root;\n  private final int splitThreshold;        // knob 1: bucket size before subdividing (4-16)\n  private final int maxDepth;              // knob 2: stop splitting — the CLUSTERING guard\n\n  boolean insert(QuadNode n, SpatialItem it) {\n    if (!n.boundary.contains(it.x(), it.y())) return false;\n    if (n.isLeaf() && (n.items.size() < splitThreshold || n.depth == maxDepth)) {\n      n.items.add(it);                     // at maxDepth the bucket may grow without limit\n      return true;\n    }\n    if (n.isLeaf()) subdivide(n);\n    for (QuadNode c : n.children) if (insert(c, it)) return true;\n    n.items.add(it);                       // straddling region: keep at the PARENT\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  private void subdivide(QuadNode n) {\n    n.children = new QuadNode[4];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) n.children[i] = new QuadNode(n.boundary.quadrant(i), n.depth + 1);\n    List<SpatialItem> old = new ArrayList<>(n.items);\n    n.items.clear();\n    for (SpatialItem it : old)             // MUST redistribute, or items strand in an internal node\n      if (!pushDown(n, it)) n.items.add(it);\n  }\n\n  void query(QuadNode n, Boundary range, List<SpatialItem> out) {\n    if (!n.boundary.intersects(range)) return;          // PRUNE: skip the whole subtree\n    for (SpatialItem it : n.items)\n      if (range.contains(it.x(), it.y())) out.add(it);\n    if (!n.isLeaf()) for (QuadNode c : n.children) query(c, range, out);\n  }\n\n  // k-NN: best-first over a min-heap of nodes keyed by minDistance(box, point);\n  // stop when the popped node's minDistance exceeds the k-th best actual distance found.\n  List<SpatialItem> nearest(double x, double y, int k) { /* two heaps: candidates + k-best */ return List.of(); }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the two knobs — split threshold, max depth, and what clustered data does",
        "A quadtree has exactly two tuning parameters and both exist for reasons you should be able to state without hand-waving. SPLITTHRESHOLD is the number of items a leaf may hold before it subdivides. Set it to 1 and every node holds a single item, which sounds pure and is a disaster: the tree becomes as deep as the data allows, every query chases pointers through many nearly-empty nodes, and the per-node overhead (a Boundary, a children array, a list header) dwarfs the payload. Set it to 1000 and the tree is shallow but every leaf query degenerates into a linear scan of a thousand items — you have built a slightly clever array. The practical band is 4 to 16, and the reasoning to give is that a leaf scan should cost about one cache line's worth of work, so the threshold should be near the number of items whose test is cheaper than another level of indirection. MAXDEPTH is the more interesting knob because it exists purely as damage control. Consider what happens without it: subdivision splits a node's box into four equal quadrants regardless of where the items are. If a thousand items sit at the SAME coordinate — a fleet parked in one depot, a rounding artefact in a GPS feed, a test dataset with a default location — then every subdivision puts all thousand into the same child, that child immediately exceeds the threshold, and it subdivides again, forever. Without a cap the recursion terminates only when floating-point coordinates stop being distinguishable, having allocated a chain of nodes tens of levels deep, each with four children, for one cluster: memory exhaustion produced by ordinary data. The cap makes the failure graceful — at maxDepth a leaf simply keeps accumulating items and queries there degrade to a linear scan of that bucket, which is bad but bounded and local. This is the general truth about the structure and it should be volunteered rather than extracted: a quadtree's shape is dictated entirely by the DATA'S SPATIAL DISTRIBUTION, not by insertion order or any balancing act. Uniformly distributed points give a beautifully balanced tree of depth about log4(n/threshold) and range queries costing roughly O(depth + result size). Real geographic data is the opposite of uniform — the population density ratio between a city centre and open water is effectively infinite — so the tree is deep and heavy over cities and a single empty node over an ocean. That is actually FINE and is the structure adapting correctly; the pathological case is not density but DEGENERACY, meaning many points at identical or near-identical coordinates, where subdivision separates nothing. The honest worst case to state: query cost is O(n) when all items land in one leaf at maxDepth, and the ordinary case is closer to O(log n + k) for a query returning k items. Two mitigations are worth naming: choosing the root boundary to actually cover the data (a root box far larger than the data wastes the first several levels on empty splits, a subtle and common inefficiency), and switching to a k-d tree when degeneracy is the norm, since a k-d tree splits at the MEDIAN and therefore divides the item count by two regardless of where the points sit."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the boundary-object problem — parent storage versus multi-leaf insertion",
        "The clean version of a quadtree stores POINTS, and a point is always inside exactly one quadrant, so insertion is unambiguous. The moment items have EXTENT — a delivery zone, a building footprint, a sprite's collision box, a geofence — the model breaks in a specific way: an item whose bounding box straddles a split line belongs to two or four children at once, and the structure has no place to put it. This is the boundary-object problem, and mishandling it is the most common source of silently wrong spatial queries, so interviewers reach for it. There are exactly two principled resolutions and each has a clear cost profile. RESOLUTION ONE, STORE AT THE PARENT: when an item does not fit entirely within any single child's boundary, keep it in the node's own bucket — the deepest node that fully contains it. The insert code above does this with its final n.items.add(it) fallback. Queries then must test the items at EVERY node along the descent, not just at leaves, which is why the query method above scans n.items before recursing rather than only at leaves. The advantages are decisive for correctness: each item is stored exactly ONCE, so remove is a single deletion, an update is a single move, and a query can never return the same item twice. The cost is that items straddling high-level split lines — most damagingly, anything crossing the CENTRE of the root, which is a single line through the middle of your map — get stuck near the root and are examined by nearly every query. In a city-centre coordinate system that can be a surprising number of items, and the standard partial fix is to choose the root's origin so the busiest area is not on a major split line, or to allow slightly overlapping (loose) child boundaries so an item can descend if it only slightly crosses. RESOLUTION TWO, INSERT INTO EVERY OVERLAPPING LEAF: push the item down into all children whose boundary intersects its box, so a large item may appear in many leaves. Queries then reach items at leaf level uniformly, and no item is stranded near the root — the query path is more predictable. The costs are real and must be stated: DUPLICATION means memory grows with how many leaves each item spans (a large region can multiply substantially), removal must find and delete every copy (which is why the item-to-nodes index becomes a list rather than a single reference), and — the critical one — a range query can now encounter the SAME item through several leaves, so results MUST be de-duplicated, either with a HashSet on item id or with a per-query visit stamp on the item (an integer compared against the current query id, which avoids allocating a set per query and is the trick used in game engines). Forgetting the de-duplication produces a query that reports the same driver three times, which is the kind of bug that survives unit tests written with small non-overlapping fixtures. Choosing between them: parent storage is right when items are small relative to cells and updates are frequent (each move is one operation), which describes moving vehicles with a small bounding box; multi-leaf insertion is right when queries dominate updates and item sizes vary widely, which describes static map features and rendering — and when items have significant extent, the honest answer is that an R-TREE, whose nodes bound their contents rather than partitioning fixed space, is the structure actually designed for the job."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: range and nearest-neighbour queries, pruning, and the true-distance filter",
        "The RANGE QUERY is three lines and the middle one is the entire value of the data structure. Line one: if this node's boundary does not intersect the query rectangle, RETURN — that single test discards an entire subtree, possibly containing a large share of the dataset, at the cost of four comparisons. Line two: test the items held at this node against the range (needed at every level under the parent-storage model, not only at leaves). Line three: recurse into all four children, each of which applies line one to itself. The property that makes this sub-linear is that a small query rectangle intersects only a small number of cells at each level, so the traversal visits O(depth) nodes along the query's spatial footprint plus the nodes actually containing results — commonly written as O(log n + k) for well-distributed data, with the honest caveat that the worst case is O(n) when the tree is degenerate. Note the structural difference from a one-dimensional index, and say it explicitly because it explains why B+Trees cannot do this: a B+Tree query follows ONE root-to-leaf path, while a spatial query descends into MULTIPLE children whenever the query straddles a split, so the traversal is a subtree walk rather than a path. CIRCLE queries (the actual 'drivers within 2km' request) are done in two stages: query the circle's bounding SQUARE, then filter the candidates by true distance. Do not attempt to prune with the circle directly at every node — a box-versus-box intersect is a handful of comparisons while a box-versus-circle test is more expensive and more error-prone; take the slightly larger candidate set and filter at the end, where the cost is proportional to the answer size. The same staging applies to geographic correctness: prune on projected coordinates, then apply the exact haversine or geodesic distance to the candidates, because putting an expensive trigonometric computation inside the traversal is a real and avoidable performance mistake. NEAREST NEIGHBOUR deserves its own treatment because the naive approach is both common and bad: repeatedly running range queries with an expanding radius until enough results appear re-traverses the tree several times and needs an arbitrary starting radius. The correct algorithm is BEST-FIRST search with two heaps. A min-heap holds nodes keyed by minDistance(node.boundary, queryPoint) — the closest any item in that box could possibly be, which for an axis-aligned box is a cheap clamped-difference computation. A bounded max-heap holds the k best items found so far, and its worst element's distance is the current search radius. Pop the most promising node; if its minDistance already exceeds the current k-th best distance, STOP — everything remaining in the heap is at least that far, so the answer is proven complete. Otherwise, evaluate its items against the k-best heap and push its children. This visits each node at most once, terminates with a provable bound rather than a guessed radius, and degrades gracefully: with k=1 it is ordinary nearest neighbour, and the same skeleton with an added predicate answers 'nearest driver who is currently available', which is the follow-up interviewers actually ask. State the complexity honestly: expected O(log n + k) for well-distributed data, worst case O(n), and note that the pruning is only as good as the tree's adaptation to the data — which loops back to why clustering matters."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "QUADTREE vs K-D TREE: a quadtree splits space at fixed geometric midpoints regardless of the data, which makes insertion cheap and the structure trivially updatable, but leaves the balance entirely at the mercy of the distribution — degenerate clusters produce deep, useless chains. A k-d tree splits at the MEDIAN of one alternating dimension, so each split halves the ITEM COUNT and the tree is balanced by construction with guaranteed depth log2(n) — better worst-case queries, but rebalancing after inserts is awkward, which is why k-d trees are the choice for static or bulk-loaded point sets (and generalize to high dimensions) while quadtrees suit dynamic 2D data. QUADTREE vs R-TREE: an R-tree's nodes are bounding rectangles that GROW to contain their children rather than partitioning fixed space, so items with extent are stored exactly once with no boundary problem and no duplication — it is the structure actually designed for regions, and it is what spatial databases ship. Its cost is a much more complex insert (choosing the subtree that minimizes area enlargement, splitting nodes to minimize overlap) and the fact that sibling rectangles may OVERLAP, so a query can still descend multiple paths. QUADTREE vs GEOHASH / SPACE-FILLING CURVE: encoding a cell as a string prefix (geohash, or a Hilbert or Z-order curve index) lets an ordinary B+Tree serve spatial queries as PREFIX RANGE scans, which is why it is the standard answer for a database-backed system with no spatial extension — the trade is that curve-based locality is imperfect (two points close in space can differ early in the encoding, so a query needs several ranges plus neighbour cells) and the cell size is fixed by prefix length rather than adapting to density. TUNING: splitThreshold trades tree depth against leaf scan cost (4-16 typical); maxDepth bounds the clustering damage; the root boundary should fit the data, since an oversized root wastes the top levels on empty splits. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: high-rate MOVEMENT is the real operational problem — a remove-plus-insert per position update churns nodes and, worse, causes cell thrashing for items oscillating across a split line; the standard mitigations are hysteresis (only re-insert when the item leaves its cell by a margin), deferred rebuilds on a snapshot swapped atomically for readers, or accepting a slightly stale index; and beyond one machine the tree must be sharded by region, at which point cross-boundary queries need scatter-gather and the design has become a distributed one. Extensions worth naming: a LOOSE quadtree with slightly enlarged child boundaries to reduce parent-stranding, per-node item counts to answer density and heat-map queries without traversal, and the octree as the direct three-dimensional analogue."),
    ],
    "The quadtree is the standard spatial-index question and it usually arrives disguised as a product problem — find nearby drivers, render visible sprites, detect collisions — so the first thing being graded is whether you recognize that ordinary one-dimensional indexes cannot answer proximity, and can say why: a B+Tree on latitude returns a band around the planet, and intersecting two such bands still scans far more than the answer. From there the interview goes to mechanism. The pruning test — skip a subtree whose boundary does not intersect the query range — is the whole speedup and must be named as such. The two knobs must be justified rather than recited, especially maxDepth, whose reason for existing is that identical or near-identical coordinates would otherwise subdivide forever. The boundary-object problem is the favourite follow-up: an item straddling a split line either stays at the parent (stored once, but stranded near the root if it crosses a major line) or goes into every overlapping leaf (uniform queries, but duplication and mandatory result de-duplication). Nearest-neighbour separates candidates too, since the expected answer is a best-first traversal with a node min-heap and a k-best max-heap and a provable stopping condition, not expanding range queries. Expect a closing comparison against k-d trees, R-trees, and geohash prefix scans.",
    [
      G("no_max_depth", "Subdividing whenever a bucket exceeds the threshold, with no depth cap, and assuming the data is roughly uniform.", "Cap the depth: identical or near-identical coordinates (a parked fleet, a default GPS value) put every item into the same child forever, so unbounded subdivision allocates a deep chain of four-child nodes until floating point stops distinguishing coordinates. At maxDepth a leaf simply accumulates and degrades to a bounded local scan — graceful instead of fatal.", "recursion terminating only on bucket size; no depth field on the node; memory blowing up on real data while synthetic uniform tests pass; the root boundary sized far larger than the data, wasting the top levels."),
      G("straddling_item_ignored", "Assuming every item lands in exactly one child, so an item whose bounding box crosses a split line is silently dropped or arbitrarily assigned.", "Pick a resolution and state its cost: keep it at the deepest node that fully contains it (stored once, easy removal, but items crossing a major split line strand near the root and are examined by nearly every query — so queries must test items at every level), or insert into every overlapping leaf (uniform leaf-level queries, but duplication, multi-copy removal, and MANDATORY result de-duplication via a set or a per-query visit stamp).", "queries returning the same item several times; items with extent disappearing from results; removal deleting only one copy; subdivide() that fails to redistribute the existing bucket, stranding items in an internal node."),
      G("expanding_radius_knn", "Implementing nearest-neighbour as repeated range queries with a growing radius, starting from a guessed initial value.", "Use best-first traversal: a min-heap of nodes keyed by the minimum possible distance from the query point to the node's box, and a bounded max-heap of the k best items whose worst distance is the live pruning radius; stop when the popped node's minimum distance exceeds that — a proof of completeness rather than a guess. For circle queries, prune on the bounding box and apply the true distance only to the candidate set.", "a loop that doubles the search radius; an arbitrary initial radius constant; the tree traversed several times for one query; haversine or other expensive distance math called inside the traversal instead of on the final candidates."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.quadtree, "Quadtree spatial index class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

